<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>P4 · L2 — Motion in 1D and 2D · YouTube Solutions</title>
<style>
  :root{
    --bg:#f6f7f9; --card:#ffffff; --ink:#1c2230; --muted:#5b6472;
    --line:#e4e7ec; --head:#1f2a44; --headtxt:#ffffff;
    --exact-bg:#e7f6ec; --exact-ink:#1f7a3d;
    --sim-bg:#fff4e0; --sim-ink:#9a6300;
    --link:#1a56db; --zebra:#fafbfc;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){
    :root{
      --bg:#0f1420; --card:#161c2b; --ink:#e6e9f0; --muted:#9aa3b2;
      --line:#283048; --head:#0d1526; --headtxt:#eef2ff;
      --exact-bg:#123322; --exact-ink:#63d68f; --sim-bg:#3a2c11; --sim-ink:#f0b95a;
      --link:#6ea8fe; --zebra:#1a2130;
    }
  }
  *{box-sizing:border-box}
  body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);
    font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
    line-height:1.5;padding:32px 18px}
  .wrap{max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto}
  header{margin-bottom:22px}
  h1{font-size:1.7rem;margin:0 0 4px}
  .sub{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;margin:0 0 14px}
  .legend{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:14px 0 4px;font-size:.85rem}
  .badge{display:inline-block;padding:2px 10px;border-radius:999px;font-weight:600;font-size:.78rem;white-space:nowrap}
  .exact{background:var(--exact-bg);color:var(--exact-ink)}
  .sim{background:var(--sim-bg);color:var(--sim-ink)}
  .note{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;
    padding:12px 16px;color:var(--muted);font-size:.88rem;margin:12px 0 26px}
  h2{font-size:1.15rem;margin:30px 0 10px;padding-bottom:6px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)}
  .tablecard{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;
    box-shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.04)}
  .scroll{overflow-x:auto}
  table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.9rem;min-width:640px}
  thead th{background:var(--head);color:var(--headtxt);text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;font-weight:600;font-size:.82rem;letter-spacing:.02em}
  tbody td{padding:10px 12px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top}
  tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:var(--zebra)}
  td.num{font-weight:700;color:var(--muted);width:34px;text-align:center}
  td.topic{color:var(--muted);font-size:.84rem;white-space:nowrap}
  a{color:var(--link);text-decoration:none}
  a:hover{text-decoration:underline}
  .q{font-weight:500}
  code{background:rgba(125,125,125,.15);padding:1px 5px;border-radius:5px;font-size:.85em}
  footer{color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;margin-top:30px;text-align:center}
  /* --- Collapsible worked solutions (full-width rows) --- */
  tbody tr.solnrow{background:var(--card)!important}
  tbody tr.solnrow>td{border-top:none;padding:0 12px 4px}
  details.soln{margin:0}
  details.soln>summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--link);font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;list-style:none;display:inline-block;user-select:none;padding:6px 0}
  details.soln>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
  details.soln>summary::before{content:"▸  Show full solution"}
  details.soln[open]>summary::before{content:"▾  Hide full solution"}
  details.soln>summary:hover{text-decoration:underline}
  .solnbody{margin:2px 0 12px;padding:14px 16px;background:var(--zebra);border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:3px solid var(--exact-ink);border-radius:9px;font-size:.85rem;font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.65;max-width:860px}
  .solnbody .seg{margin:8px 0}
  .solnbody .lbl{display:inline-block;font-weight:800;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.05em;font-size:.68rem;margin-right:7px;vertical-align:1px}
  .solnbody .step{margin:5px 0 5px 4px}
  .solnbody .step .why{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.93em;margin:1px 0 0 14px}
  .solnbody code{font-size:.9em}
  .solnbody pre{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:7px;padding:9px 11px;margin:8px 0;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.5;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre;color:var(--ink)}
  .solnbody .eq{font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;background:rgba(125,125,125,.10);padding:1px 5px;border-radius:4px}
  .solnbody .trick{margin-top:10px;padding:10px 12px;background:var(--sim-bg);color:var(--sim-ink);border-radius:8px;font-size:.84rem}
  .solnbody .trick .lbl{color:var(--sim-ink)}
  .solnbody .final{margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;border-top:1px dashed var(--line);font-weight:700;color:var(--exact-ink)}
  /* --- Answer key (toggle) --- */
  .controls{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:18px 0 2px}
  .ansbtn{appearance:none;cursor:pointer;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.15);background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1f2a44,#2f4370);color:#eef2ff;font:600 13.5px/1 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Roboto,sans-serif;padding:10px 16px;border-radius:10px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(20,30,60,.18)}
  .ansbtn:hover{filter:brightness(1.09)}
  .ansbtn:active{transform:translateY(1px)}
  .controls .hint{font-size:12.5px;color:var(--muted)}
  th.ans-h{min-width:170px;text-align:left!important}
  td.ans{text-align:left;min-width:170px}
  .ansval{display:inline-block;font-weight:600;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--exact-ink);background:var(--exact-bg);border-radius:7px;padding:3px 10px;filter:blur(6px);cursor:pointer;user-select:none;transition:filter .12s;line-height:1.4}
  .ansval:hover{filter:blur(3px)}
  .ansval.revealed{filter:none;cursor:default;user-select:text}
  body.show-ans .ansval{filter:none;cursor:default;user-select:text}
  .ansval .lt{font-weight:800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.4px;margin-right:6px;color:inherit}
  .ansval.flagged{background:var(--sim-bg);color:var(--sim-ink)}
  .warn{cursor:help}
  .na{color:var(--muted)}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">
<header>
  <h1>P4 · L2 — Motion in 1D and 2D</h1>
  <p class="sub">Practice Problems mapped to YouTube solution videos</p>
  <div class="legend">
    <span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.82rem">video solves the same question (same numbers)</span>
    <span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.82rem">same method / concept, different numbers</span>
  </div>
  <div class="note"><b>Scope:</b> Practice problems only (Suggested Problems, Level: Board, and Levels 1–6). Solved examples and Quick Recap are excluded.</div>
  <div class="controls">
    <button id="ansBtn" class="ansbtn" type="button">🔑 Show Answers</button>
    <span class="hint">Answers (letter <b>+</b> the option text/value exactly as printed in the P4L2 source PDF, keyed from the ICAD P-V1 Answer Key) are hidden by default — click to reveal all, or click any single blurred answer to reveal just that one. ⚠ marks a row where the key, the printed book, or our own write-up disagree — hover it for the detail.</span>
  </div>
</header>

<h2>Suggested Problems</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">What is the SI unit of acceleration? (a) m/s (b) km/h (c) m/s² (d) cm/s²</td><td class="topic">SI unit of acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FtYxdu42skk" target="_blank">The unit of acceleration in the SI system is</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y7SjCozNBPc" target="_blank">SI unit of acceleration is</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>m/s²</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">A particle starts with velocity 10 m/s and acceleration 2 m/s². Find the velocity (in m/s) at t = 4 s</td><td class="topic">v = u + at</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fdRT8LMxD9A" target="_blank">Acceleration question with initial and final velocity</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHdBeRz_T68" target="_blank">Derive v = u + at (equation of motion derivation)</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">18</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Acceleration is the <b>rate of change of velocity</b>. When it is <i>constant</i>, the velocity does not merely change — it changes by the <b>same amount every second</b>. So the velocity gained over a stretch of time is just "how much per second" × "how many seconds," which is added on top of whatever velocity the particle already had. That single idea is the whole of the first equation of motion; no integration is needed once a is constant.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">v = u + a·t</span> — v = final velocity (m s⁻¹), u = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), a = constant acceleration (m s⁻²), t = time elapsed (s). <span class="why">Units check: a·t has m s⁻² × s = m s⁻¹, which matches u and v, so the sum is dimensionally legal.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> a is constant over the whole interval and the motion is along a straight line, so all quantities can be treated as signed scalars along one axis.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. List the data with units: u = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b>, a = <b>2 m s⁻²</b>, t = <b>4 s</b>; unknown v.<span class="why">Both u and a are positive, so the particle speeds up along its direction of motion.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Choose the equation linking u, a, t and v — that is v = u + a·t.<span class="why">Displacement is neither given nor asked, so the first equation of motion is the direct route.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Compute the velocity <i>gained</i>: a·t = 2 m s⁻² × 4 s = <b>8 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">Gaining 2 m s⁻¹ every second for 4 s gives 2+2+2+2 = 8 m s⁻¹.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Add it to the initial velocity: v = 10 m s⁻¹ + 8 m s⁻¹ = <b>18 m s⁻¹</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Sanity check: v &gt; u as expected for positive acceleration, and 18 m s⁻¹ is a plausible everyday speed.</div>
<pre>
 t (s)    velocity v = u + a·t          value
  0       10 + 2(0)  = 10 + 0           10 m/s   &lt;-- start
  1       10 + 2(1)  = 10 + 2           12 m/s
  2       10 + 2(2)  = 10 + 4           14 m/s
  3       10 + 2(3)  = 10 + 6           16 m/s
  4       10 + 2(4)  = 10 + 8           18 m/s   &lt;-- ANSWER
                                        (+2 m/s each second)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Pick the equation of motion by spotting <b>which of the five symbols (u, v, a, t, s) is missing</b>. Here s never appears, and <span class="eq">v = u + at</span> is the only equation without s — a one-second selection with no algebra. Trap: if a were a <i>retardation</i>, you must substitute it as a <b>negative</b> number (a = −2 m s⁻²), not subtract a positive one by hand — the sign belongs in the substitution.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 18 m s⁻¹.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A train moving with uniform acceleration: the two ends of the train pass a point on the track with velocities v₁ and v₂ (v₁ = v₂ = 2 m/s). Find the velocity with which the middle point of the train passes the same point</td><td class="topic">Velocity of the midpoint from end velocities</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IOWXrFwe-PA" target="_blank">A train is moving with uniform acceleration. The two ends of the train pass through a point on t...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval flagged">2 <span class="warn" title="Answer-key value 2 (m/s). Confirmed against the PDF (p.153): the book really does say the train moves 'with uniform acceleration' and then fixes V₁ = V₂ = 2 m/s — but equal velocities at the two ends force a = 0, contradicting 'uniform acceleration'. The question is self-contradictory as printed; the key's 2 m/s is what the degenerate a = 0 case gives (and also what √((v₁²+v₂²)/2) gives here). Book defect, confirmed.">⚠</span></span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">A car starts from rest and moves with constant acceleration. Ratio of the distance covered in the nth second to that covered in n seconds: (a) 2/n² − 1/n (b) 2/n² + 1/n (c) 2/n − 1/n² (d) 2/n + 1/n²</td><td class="topic">Distance in nth second vs n seconds</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yhqp33Yqi-0" target="_blank">A car starts from rest and moves with constant acceleration. The ratio of the distance covered in the nth second to that covered in `n` seconds is</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>2/n − 1/n²</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">A car accelerates from rest at a constant rate α, then decelerates at a constant rate β and comes to rest. If total time elapsed is t, maximum velocity acquired: (a) (α²−β²)/αβ · t (b) (α²+β²)/αβ · t (c) (α+β)/αβ · t (d) αβ/(α+β) · t</td><td class="topic">Max velocity, two-phase motion</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AfxxnH6OizA" target="_blank">A car accelerates from rest at a constant rate α for some time after which it decelerates at a cons...</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HUJT1xQMVws" target="_blank">A car accelerates from rest at a constant rate ( α ) for some time after which it deceler...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>αβ/(α + β) · t</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level: Board</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A car moves on a straight road with uniform acceleration. Speed (m/s): 5,10,15,20,25,30 at Time (s): 0,10,20,30,40,50. Draw the speed-time graph (convenient scale) and calculate (i) acceleration (ii) distance travelled in 10 s</td><td class="topic">Speed-time graph → acceleration &amp; distance</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IYomkCNaskE" target="_blank">Speed-Time Graph Explained! (Find Distance, Acceleration &amp; More)</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">0.5 m/s², 75 m</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A speed–time graph converts kinematics into geometry. Two readings come straight off the picture: the <b>slope</b> of the line is the acceleration (rise = change in speed, run = change in time), and the <b>area under the line</b> up to any instant is the distance covered in that interval. Here the speed rises by exactly 5 m s⁻¹ every 10 s, so the plot is a <i>straight line</i> — the signature of uniform acceleration — and both readings are easy.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> (i) <span class="eq">a = Δv ÷ Δt = (v₂ − v₁) ÷ (t₂ − t₁)</span> in m s⁻², the slope. (ii) <span class="eq">s = area under the v–t graph = ½ × (u + v) × t</span> in m — the area of a trapezium of parallel sides u and v (m s⁻¹) and width t (s). <span class="why">Units: m s⁻¹ × s = m, so an area on a v–t graph is genuinely a distance.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the tabulated points lie on one straight line (verified below), so a is constant and the trapezium rule is exact.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Plot/inspect the data — speed 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 m s⁻¹ at times 0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 s.<span class="why">Every 10 s step raises the speed by exactly 5 m s⁻¹, so the points lie on a straight line: acceleration is uniform.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. <b>(i) Acceleration</b> — take the slope over the full run: Δv = 30 m s⁻¹ − 5 m s⁻¹ = 25 m s⁻¹; Δt = 50 s − 0 s = 50 s.</div>
<div class="step">3. a = 25 m s⁻¹ ÷ 50 s = <b>0.5 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">Cross-check on one small step: (10 − 5) ÷ (10 − 0) = 5 ÷ 10 = 0.5 m s⁻² — the same, confirming the line.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. <b>(ii) Distance in the first 10 s</b> — the region under the graph from t = 0 to t = 10 s is a trapezium with parallel sides u = 5 m s⁻¹ and v = 10 m s⁻¹, width t = 10 s.</div>
<div class="step">5. s = ½ × (5 m s⁻¹ + 10 m s⁻¹) × 10 s = ½ × 15 m s⁻¹ × 10 s = <b>75 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">6. Cross-check with s = ut + ½at² = (5)(10) + ½(0.5)(10)² = 50 m + 25 m = <b>75 m</b> ✓ — both routes agree.</div>
<pre>
 v (m/s)
  30 |                                        * (50,30)
  25 |                               * (40,25)
  20 |                      * (30,20)
  15 |             * (20,15)
  10 |    * (10,10)
   5 |*(0,5)
     +----+----+----+----+----+---- t (s)
     0   10   20   30   40   50

 slope = (30-5)/(50-0) = 25/50 = 0.5 m/s²
 area 0..10 s = ½(5+10)(10) = 75 m
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> On any v–t graph: <b>slope = acceleration, area = displacement</b> — memorise the pair and most graph questions become geometry with no equations at all. For the area of the first strip use the trapezium ½(u+v)t rather than ut + ½at²; it needs no value of a and is faster. Trap: the graph starts at <b>5 m s⁻¹, not at the origin</b>, so the region is a trapezium — treating it as a triangle (½×10×10 = 50 m) loses the rectangular base and is the standard wrong answer.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (i) acceleration = 0.5 m s⁻² ; (ii) distance in 10 s = 75 m.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">Speed of a car reduced from 90 km/hr to 36 km/hr in 5 s. Find the distance travelled during this interval</td><td class="topic">Retardation, s = (v²−u²)/2a</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fAaLDXFtkL8" target="_blank">v² − u² = 2as Equation Practice Example</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">87.5 m</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Two separate ideas do this problem. First, <b>km h⁻¹ is not an SI unit</b> — every kinematic equation assumes metres and seconds, so both speeds must be converted before any substitution. Second, the car is <i>slowing down</i>, so the acceleration is <b>negative</b> (a retardation); the sign is not decoration — it is what makes the algebra return a positive distance. With u, v and t known, the cleanest route is the average-velocity form, which needs no value of a at all.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> Conversion: <span class="eq">1 km h⁻¹ = 1000 m ÷ 3600 s = 5/18 m s⁻¹</span>. Then <span class="eq">s = ½ × (u + v) × t</span> — distance (m) = mean of initial and final velocity (m s⁻¹) × time (s), valid <i>only</i> for constant acceleration. Equivalent route: <span class="eq">a = (v − u) ÷ t</span> (m s⁻²) then <span class="eq">s = (v² − u²) ÷ (2a)</span>. <b>Assumption:</b> the retardation is uniform over the 5 s and the motion is in a straight line.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Convert the initial speed: u = 90 km h⁻¹ × (5/18) = 450/18 = <b>25 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">Multiplying by 5/18 is the same as ×1000 m then ÷3600 s.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Convert the final speed: v = 36 km h⁻¹ × (5/18) = 180/18 = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b>. Time t = <b>5 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. Find the acceleration: a = (v − u) ÷ t = (10 m s⁻¹ − 25 m s⁻¹) ÷ 5 s = (−15 m s⁻¹) ÷ 5 s = <b>−3 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">The minus sign confirms a retardation of magnitude 3 m s⁻².</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Use the average-velocity form: s = ½ × (25 m s⁻¹ + 10 m s⁻¹) × 5 s.</div>
<div class="step">5. s = ½ × 35 m s⁻¹ × 5 s = 17.5 m s⁻¹ × 5 s = <b>87.5 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">6. Cross-check with s = (v² − u²) ÷ (2a) = (10² − 25²) ÷ (2 × −3) = (100 − 625) ÷ (−6) = (−525) ÷ (−6) = <b>87.5 m</b> ✓.<span class="why">Both a negative numerator and a negative denominator — the two minus signs cancel to give a positive distance, as they must.</span></div>
<pre>
 quantity   given          SI value      note
 u          90 km/hr       25 m/s        ×5/18
 v          36 km/hr       10 m/s        ×5/18
 t          5 s            5 s
 a          —              −3 m/s²       (10−25)/5, retardation
 s = ½(u+v)t = ½(25+10)(5) = 87.5 m
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Memorise <b>×5/18 for km h⁻¹ → m s⁻¹</b> and <b>×18/5 for the reverse</b>; the everyday anchors 36 km h⁻¹ = 10 m s⁻¹, 72 km h⁻¹ = 20 m s⁻¹, 90 km h⁻¹ = 25 m s⁻¹ let you convert on sight. When <b>u, v and t</b> are the givens, jump straight to <span class="eq">s = ½(u+v)t</span> — it skips finding a entirely. Trap: substituting 90 and 36 directly gives a nonsense distance in mixed units; convert <i>first</i>, always.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 87.5 m.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A scooter moving at 12 m/s is stopped by brakes producing uniform retardation of 0.60 m/s². Distance covered before it stops?</td><td class="topic">Stopping distance</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5zb6I_huqD4" target="_blank">A scooter moving at a speed of 10 m/s is stopped by applying brakes which...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">120 m</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> This is the classic <b>stopping-distance</b> problem. The phrase "is stopped" is the hidden data: it fixes the <b>final velocity at v = 0</b>. Time is neither given nor wanted, so the equation that omits t — the third equation of motion, v² = u² + 2as — connects the two speeds directly to the distance. Note the physically important consequence: because u appears <i>squared</i>, stopping distance grows with the <b>square</b> of speed, not in proportion to it.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">v² = u² + 2·a·s</span>, rearranged to <span class="eq">s = (v² − u²) ÷ (2a)</span>. Units: v², u² in m² s⁻²; a in m s⁻²; so (m² s⁻²) ÷ (m s⁻²) = m ✓. With v = 0 this collapses to the memorable form <span class="eq">s = u² ÷ (2|a|)</span>. <b>Assumption:</b> the retardation is uniform at 0.60 m s⁻² right up to the stop, and the path is straight.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Extract the data with signs: u = <b>12 m s⁻¹</b>, v = <b>0 m s⁻¹</b> (it stops), a = <b>−0.60 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">"Retardation of 0.60 m s⁻²" means the acceleration opposes the motion, so it enters the algebra as −0.60 m s⁻².</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Pick the equation with no t in it: v² = u² + 2as.<span class="why">Neither the given data nor the question mentions time, so eliminate it.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Substitute with units: (0 m s⁻¹)² = (12 m s⁻¹)² + 2 × (−0.60 m s⁻²) × s.</div>
<div class="step">4. Evaluate the squares: 0 = 144 m² s⁻² − 1.20 m s⁻² × s.</div>
<div class="step">5. Solve for s: s = 144 m² s⁻² ÷ 1.20 m s⁻² = <b>120 m</b>.<span class="why">Dividing m² s⁻² by m s⁻² leaves metres — dimensionally sound.</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Sanity check via time: the scooter stops after t = u ÷ |a| = 12 ÷ 0.60 = 20 s, and s = ½(u+v)t = ½(12)(20) = <b>120 m</b> ✓ — a gentle brake, hence a long stop.</div>
<pre>
 u = 12 m/s          v = 0
   |------------------ s = ? ------------------|
   *===========================================>| stop
   a = −0.60 m/s² (uniform retardation)

 s = u²/(2|a|) = (12)²/(2 × 0.60) = 144/1.20 = 120 m
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Burn in <span class="eq">s = u²/(2|a|)</span> for anything that "comes to rest" — it turns most braking MCQs into one division. Because it scales as <b>u²</b>, <b>doubling the speed quadruples the stopping distance</b>; examiners love this ratio (asked as "speed doubled ⇒ distance ×4"). Trap: forgetting the minus sign on a retardation returns a negative distance — if s comes out negative, you dropped the sign, not physics.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 120 m.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">An object starts with u = 10 m/s and reaches v = 30 m/s in 10 s. Find acceleration. (a) 1 m/s² (b) 2 m/s² (c) 3 m/s² (d) 4 m/s²</td><td class="topic">a = (v−u)/t</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHdBeRz_T68" target="_blank">Derive v = u + at (equation of motion derivation)</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="na" title="No key entry — and none should exist. Verified against the PDF (pp.153–154): the book's LEVEL: BOARD section contains only THREE questions (Q1–Q3); the key likewise prints only 3. This row reproduces the book's LEVEL-2 Q4, which our page already lists correctly under Level 2. OUR error — a spurious duplicate row, not a book defect.">—</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Acceleration is defined as the <b>change in velocity divided by the time taken</b> for that change — literally "how many m s⁻¹ are gained each second." It is a <i>rate</i>, so what matters is not how fast the object is going but how fast its velocity is <b>changing</b>. Here the object gains 20 m s⁻¹ over 10 s, so it must be gaining 2 m s⁻¹ in every single second.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a = (v − u) ÷ t</span> — the rearranged first equation of motion. Units: (m s⁻¹ − m s⁻¹) ÷ s = m s⁻¹ ÷ s = <b>m s⁻²</b> ✓, which is exactly the SI unit of acceleration. <b>Assumption:</b> the acceleration is uniform over the 10 s, so this average value is also the value at every instant.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Write the data with units: u = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b>, v = <b>30 m s⁻¹</b>, t = <b>10 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">2. Find the change in velocity: Δv = v − u = 30 m s⁻¹ − 10 m s⁻¹ = <b>20 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">Subtract the initial from the final — a positive result means the object sped up.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Divide by the time taken: a = 20 m s⁻¹ ÷ 10 s = <b>2 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">m s⁻¹ ÷ s gives m s⁻², confirming the quantity really is an acceleration.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Match to the options: 2 m s⁻² is option <b>(b)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Verify forwards: v = u + at = 10 m s⁻¹ + (2 m s⁻²)(10 s) = 10 + 20 = <b>30 m s⁻¹</b> ✓ — reproduces the given final velocity.</div>
<pre>
 u = 10 m/s  ──── 10 s of uniform a ────>  v = 30 m/s
 Δv = 30 − 10 = 20 m/s
 a  = Δv/t = 20/10 = 2 m/s²   → option (b)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Read <b>a = Δv/t as "velocity gained per second"</b> — 20 m s⁻¹ shared over 10 s is 2 m s⁻¹ each second, answerable mentally with no equation written. Trap: never use <b>v/t</b> (30/10 = 3 m s⁻², option c) — that is the tempting distractor planted for students who forget to subtract u. Acceleration depends on the <i>change</i> in velocity, never on the velocity itself.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (b) 2 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">A body moving with constant acceleration covers 20 m in 4 s. If u = 0, find a. (a) 2 m/s² (b) 2.5 m/s² (c) 3 m/s² (d) 3.5 m/s²</td><td class="topic">s = ut + ½at²</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFcEBISYCVA" target="_blank">Acceleration Calculations - Final Velocity After Any Displacement</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="na" title="No key entry — and none should exist. Verified against the PDF (pp.153–154): the book's LEVEL: BOARD section contains only THREE questions (Q1–Q3). This row reproduces the book's LEVEL-2 Q5, which our page already lists correctly under Level 2. OUR error — a spurious duplicate row, not a book defect.">—</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> When a body <b>starts from rest</b>, the first term of the displacement equation vanishes (u = 0), and the distance covered depends on the acceleration and on the <b>square of the time</b> alone. This is the equation that links s, u, a and t while leaving out the final velocity v — exactly the right tool when the question gives a distance and a time and asks for a.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">s = u·t + ½·a·t²</span> — s = displacement (m), u = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), a = constant acceleration (m s⁻²), t = time (s). <span class="why">Units: u·t → m s⁻¹ × s = m; ½at² → m s⁻² × s² = m — both terms are metres, so they may legally be added.</span> With u = 0 this reduces to <span class="eq">s = ½·a·t²</span>, i.e. <span class="eq">a = 2s ÷ t²</span>. <b>Assumption:</b> uniform acceleration in a straight line, timed from the instant of release.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with units: s = <b>20 m</b>, t = <b>4 s</b>, u = <b>0 m s⁻¹</b> (given), unknown a.</div>
<div class="step">2. Substitute into s = ut + ½at²: 20 m = (0 m s⁻¹)(4 s) + ½ × a × (4 s)².</div>
<div class="step">3. The first term dies because u = 0: (0)(4) = <b>0 m</b>.<span class="why">Starting from rest means no distance is contributed by an initial velocity.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Evaluate t²: (4 s)² = <b>16 s²</b>, so 20 m = ½ × a × 16 s² = a × 8 s².</div>
<div class="step">5. Solve: a = 20 m ÷ 8 s² = <b>2.5 m s⁻²</b> — option <b>(b)</b>.<span class="why">Dividing m by s² gives m s⁻², the correct unit for acceleration.</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Check backwards: s = ½ × 2.5 m s⁻² × (4 s)² = ½ × 2.5 × 16 = <b>20 m</b> ✓.</div>
<pre>
 given: u = 0, s = 20 m, t = 4 s
 s = ut + ½at²
 20 = 0(4) + ½·a·(4)²
 20 = 8a
  a = 20/8 = 2.5 m/s²      → option (b)

 [wrong route] "a = s/t² = 20/16 = 1.25" — forgets the ½
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> For anything released <b>from rest</b>, collapse the equation on sight to <span class="eq">a = 2s/t²</span> — one substitution, no algebra: 2(20)/16 = 2.5 m s⁻². Trap: the factor <b>½</b> is the most-dropped symbol in kinematics; omitting it halves the answer to 1.25 m s⁻², and forgetting to <i>square</i> the time gives 5 m s⁻² — both are stock distractors. Note s ∝ t², so <b>doubling the time quadruples the distance</b>.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (b) 2.5 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 1</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">What is instantaneous acceleration? (a) Acceleration over a week (b) Acceleration calculated over a time interval (c) Acceleration at a specific instant in time (d) Acceleration over a distance</td><td class="topic">Instantaneous acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlWTlivvtpk" target="_blank">Velocity-time graph to compute instantaneous acceleration, average velocity from a velocity graph</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>Acceleration at a specific instant in time</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> There are two distinct accelerations in kinematics. <b>Average acceleration</b> is Δv/Δt over a finite stretch of time — it smears out everything that happened inside the interval. <b>Instantaneous acceleration</b> is what you get when that interval is shrunk towards zero: the acceleration <b>at one particular instant</b>. The word "instantaneous" means precisely "at an instant," which is why a limit (or a derivative) is needed to define it at all. On a v–t graph, average acceleration is the slope of the <i>chord</i>; instantaneous acceleration is the slope of the <i>tangent</i> at that point.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a_inst = lim(Δt→0) Δv/Δt = dv/dt</span> — the time-derivative of velocity, in <b>m s⁻²</b>; compare <span class="eq">a_avg = Δv/Δt</span> over a finite Δt. <span class="why">Both carry the same unit m s⁻²; only the size of the interval distinguishes them, and Δt → 0 is what pins the value to a single instant.</span></div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Parse the key word: "instantaneous" = at one specific <b>instant</b> of time, not spread over a span.</div>
<div class="step">2. Formally it is the limit of Δv/Δt as Δt → 0, i.e. the derivative dv/dt evaluated at that instant.<span class="why">Shrinking the interval to zero removes any averaging, leaving the value at a single moment.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Read this off a v–t graph as the <b>slope of the tangent</b> at that point (the chord's slope would give the average instead).</div>
<div class="step">4. Therefore the description "acceleration at a specific instant in time" — option <b>(c)</b> — is correct.</div>
<div class="step">5. Eliminate the rest: (a) "over a week" and (b) "calculated over a time interval" both describe <b>average</b> acceleration over a finite Δt; (d) "over a distance" is meaningless — acceleration is a rate with respect to <i>time</i>, never distance.</div>
<pre>
 v |            ,-'  tangent slope  = INSTANTANEOUS a at P
   |         ,-* P                    (Δt → 0)
   |     _,-' |
   |  _-'     |   chord slope A→B = AVERAGE a over Δt
   | *A-------*B
   +---------------- t
        |&lt;- Δt ->|
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Translate the vocabulary once and these MCQs are free: <b>"instantaneous" ⇒ tangent / derivative / Δt→0</b>; <b>"average" ⇒ chord / Δv over a finite Δt</b>. The same split applies to velocity (v_inst = dx/dt vs v_avg = Δx/Δt). Trap: option (d) "over a distance" — acceleration is <i>always</i> a rate of change with respect to time; a change per unit distance (dv/dx) is a different quantity entirely.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (c) Acceleration at a specific instant in time.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">Which best describes uniformly accelerated motion? (a) Velocity increases in unequal amounts (b) Acceleration is zero (c) Acceleration is variable (d) Velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals</td><td class="topic">Definition of uniform acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0kQrz4dfxDw" target="_blank">Understanding Uniformly Accelerated Motion</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kvFGJ11Qft0" target="_blank">Motion with Uniform Acceleration // HSC Physics</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>Velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> "Uniformly accelerated" does <b>not</b> mean the velocity is uniform — it means the <i>acceleration</i> is uniform, i.e. constant in magnitude and direction. Since acceleration is the velocity gained per second, holding it constant forces the velocity to change by the <b>same amount in every equal interval of time</b>: +2 m s⁻¹ in the 1st second, +2 m s⁻¹ in the 2nd, and so on. That equal-changes-in-equal-times signature is the defining behaviour, and it is what makes the v–t graph a straight line.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a = Δv/Δt = constant</span> (m s⁻²) ⟺ <span class="eq">Δv = a·Δt</span> is the same for every equal Δt. <span class="why">If a is fixed, then for any two equal time slices Δt the product a·Δt is identical, so the velocity increments are identical.</span> Consequence: <span class="eq">v = u + at</span> is linear in t — a straight-line v–t graph of slope a.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Start from the definition: uniform acceleration means a stays constant in size <i>and</i> direction.</div>
<div class="step">2. Rearrange: Δv = a·Δt. With a constant, equal time intervals Δt must give equal velocity changes Δv.<span class="why">e.g. a = 2 m s⁻² gives Δv = 2 m s⁻¹ in every 1 s, without exception.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Restated: the <b>velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals</b> — exactly option <b>(d)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. Reject the distractors: (a) unequal changes in equal times ⇒ a is <i>varying</i>, so not uniform; (b) a = 0 ⇒ no acceleration at all (uniform <i>velocity</i>, a different thing); (c) "acceleration is variable" is the direct opposite of uniform.</div>
<pre>
 uniform a = 2 m/s², starting from u = 4 m/s
 t (s)   0     1     2     3     4
 v(m/s)  4     6     8    10    12
 Δv        +2    +2    +2    +2    &lt;-- equal in equal times ✓
 (v–t graph: straight line, slope = a = 2 m/s²)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Map the words onto the graph and the option picks itself: <b>uniform velocity ⇒ v–t is horizontal (a = 0)</b>; <b>uniform acceleration ⇒ v–t is a sloping straight line</b>; <b>non-uniform acceleration ⇒ v–t is curved</b>. Trap: "uniform" attaches to the noun that follows it — uniform <i>acceleration</i> allows the velocity to change (it must!); only uniform <i>velocity</i> forbids it. Option (b) baits exactly that confusion.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (d) Velocity changes by equal amounts in equal time intervals.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">Which equation represents: final velocity = initial velocity + acceleration × time? (a) v = u + at (b) s = ut + ½at² (c) v² = u² + 2as (d) a = (v−u)/t</td><td class="topic">Identify v = u + at</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHdBeRz_T68" target="_blank">Derive v = u + at (equation of motion derivation)</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>v = u + at</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> This asks you to translate a sentence into symbols. "Final velocity = initial velocity + acceleration × time" maps word-for-word onto the <b>first equation of motion</b>. Its physical content: acceleration is the velocity gained per second, so over t seconds the body gains a·t of velocity, which simply adds to the u it began with. The three standard equations differ by which symbol they leave out — recognising that is the fastest way to identify any of them.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">v = u + a·t</span> — v = final velocity (m s⁻¹), u = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), a = acceleration (m s⁻²), t = time (s). <span class="why">Units confirm the reading: a·t = m s⁻² × s = m s⁻¹, a velocity, so "acceleration × time" is literally the velocity gained.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> a is constant, otherwise the product a·t is not the velocity gained.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Translate each phrase: "final velocity" → v; "initial velocity" → u; "acceleration × time" → a·t.</div>
<div class="step">2. Assemble the sentence: v = u + a·t — which is option <b>(a)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. Confirm dimensionally: a·t has units m s⁻² × s = m s⁻¹, matching u and v, so all three terms are velocities ✓.<span class="why">A statement that adds a velocity to a velocity to give a velocity is dimensionally sound.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Check the distractors: (b) s = ut + ½at² gives a <b>displacement</b>, not a velocity; (c) v² = u² + 2as relates velocity <i>squared</i> to displacement (no time in it); (d) a = (v−u)/t is a true statement but it is the same relation <b>rearranged for a</b>, so it does not read "final velocity = …".</div>
<div class="step">5. Note (a) and (d) are algebraically equivalent — the question asks which expresses <b>v</b> as the subject, so (a) is the intended match.</div>
<pre>
 words:    final velocity = initial velocity + acceleration × time
 symbols:        v         =        u        +        a      ×  t
 units:       m s⁻¹        =      m s⁻¹      +   (m s⁻² × s = m s⁻¹)  ✓

 equation            omits    use when
 v = u + at            s      no distance involved   ← this one
 s = ut + ½at²         v      final velocity unknown
 v² = u² + 2as         t      time unknown
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Index the three equations by <b>the symbol each one is missing</b> — v = u + at has no s, s = ut + ½at² has no v, v² = u² + 2as has no t. Spot the quantity that is neither given nor asked, and the correct equation is chosen in one second. Trap: (d) is <i>not</i> wrong physics, just the wrong subject — when two options are equivalent, re-read what the question made the subject of the sentence.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (a) v = u + at.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">Which calculus operation gives displacement when applied to velocity? (a) Multiplication (b) Differentiation (c) Division (d) Integration</td><td class="topic">Integration of velocity</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WABpcU0mHMU" target="_blank">How to use calculus in Kinematics - Displacement, Velocity &amp; Acceleration</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>Integration</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Kinematics has a <b>calculus ladder</b>: displacement → velocity → acceleration going <i>down</i> by differentiation, and acceleration → velocity → displacement coming back <i>up</i> by integration. Since velocity is <b>defined</b> as the derivative of displacement (v = dx/dt), recovering displacement from velocity means undoing a derivative — which is integration. Physically, integrating v over time is just adding up (velocity × tiny time) over every instant, i.e. accumulating the small distances into a total.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">x = ∫ v dt</span>, or over an interval <span class="eq">Δx = ∫(t₁→t₂) v dt</span>. Units: v dt = m s⁻¹ × s = <b>m</b> ✓, so the integral genuinely returns a displacement. <span class="why">This is also why the <b>area under a v–t graph</b> is the displacement — an integral is an area.</span> The inverse operation is <span class="eq">v = dx/dt</span> (differentiation).</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Recall the definition: velocity is the rate of change of displacement, v = dx/dt.<span class="why">Velocity was <i>obtained from</i> displacement by differentiating.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. To go backwards from v to x you must undo the derivative — the inverse of differentiation is <b>integration</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. So x = ∫ v dt, which is option <b>(d)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. Check the units: (m s⁻¹) × (s) = m — integrating velocity over time yields metres ✓.</div>
<div class="step">5. Sanity check on a simple case: constant v = 5 m s⁻¹ over 3 s gives ∫₀³ 5 dt = 5 × 3 = <b>15 m</b> — the familiar distance = speed × time, exactly as an integral should reproduce.</div>
<div class="step">6. Reject the rest: (b) differentiation goes the <i>wrong way</i> (v → a); (a) multiplication only works if v is constant — a special case, not the general operation; (c) division is not an inverse of a derivative at all.</div>
<pre>
                differentiate  (d/dt)
   x  ──────────────────────────────────>  v  ──────────>  a
 (m)   &lt;──────────────────────────────────  (m/s)  &lt;──────  (m/s²)
                 integrate  (∫ ... dt)

 x = ∫ v dt   [m/s · s = m]  ✓   ← displacement from velocity
 a = dv/dt    [m/s ÷ s = m/s²]
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Fix the ladder <b>x ⇄ v ⇄ a</b> once: <b>going down = differentiate, going up = integrate</b>. It instantly answers every "which operation" MCQ and reminds you that <b>area under v–t = displacement</b> while <b>slope of v–t = acceleration</b>. Trap: option (a) multiplication is right <i>only</i> for constant velocity (x = vt); the moment v depends on t you must integrate.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (d) Integration.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">Check the only correct statement: (a) constant velocity yet varying speed (b) constant speed yet varying velocity (c) constant speed cannot have acceleration (d) motion under a force must always have work done on it</td><td class="topic">Speed vs velocity, acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IfYub3QOfuI" target="_blank">Can a body have a constant speed but a varying velocity?</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">b</span>A body has a constant speed but it can have a varying velocity.</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 2</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A body starts from rest and travels 40 m in 4 s under uniform acceleration. Find the acceleration. (a) 2.5 m/s² (b) 4 m/s² (c) 5 m/s² (d) 10 m/s²</td><td class="topic">s = ut + ½at²</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1lCFjmbLpWA" target="_blank">A body is moving with a uniform acceleration coverss 40 m in the first 4 s and 120 m in next 4s...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>5 m/s²</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A body released <b>from rest</b> under constant acceleration covers a distance governed entirely by a and t. Because u = 0 kills the ut term, the distance depends on the <b>square of the time</b>: s = ½at². Given a distance and the time taken, the acceleration follows by rearranging — no velocity needs to be found anywhere.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">s = u·t + ½·a·t²</span> — s in m, u in m s⁻¹, a in m s⁻², t in s. <span class="why">Each term must be a length: u·t = m s⁻¹ × s = m, and ½at² = m s⁻² × s² = m ✓.</span> With u = 0: <span class="eq">s = ½at²</span> ⟹ <span class="eq">a = 2s ÷ t²</span>. <b>Assumption:</b> the acceleration is uniform throughout the 4 s and the motion is rectilinear.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with units: u = <b>0 m s⁻¹</b> ("starts from rest"), s = <b>40 m</b>, t = <b>4 s</b>; find a.</div>
<div class="step">2. Substitute into s = ut + ½at²: 40 m = (0 m s⁻¹)(4 s) + ½ × a × (4 s)².</div>
<div class="step">3. The ut term vanishes: (0 m s⁻¹)(4 s) = <b>0 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. Square the time: (4 s)² = <b>16 s²</b>. So 40 m = ½ × a × 16 s² = a × <b>8 s²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Solve: a = 40 m ÷ 8 s² = <b>5 m s⁻²</b> — option <b>(c)</b>.<span class="why">m ÷ s² = m s⁻², the right unit for acceleration.</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Verify: s = ½ × 5 m s⁻² × (4 s)² = ½ × 5 × 16 = <b>40 m</b> ✓. Final speed v = at = 5 × 4 = 20 m s⁻¹, and ½(0+20)(4) = 40 m ✓ — consistent by a second route.</div>
<pre>
 u = 0, s = 40 m, t = 4 s
 s   = ut + ½at²
 40  = 0(4) + ½·a·(4)²
 40  = 8a
  a  = 40/8 = 5 m/s²        → option (c)

 distractor traps:
   a = s/t²  = 40/16 = 2.5 m/s²  (a) — the ½ was dropped
   a = 2s/t  = 80/4  = 20        — time not squared
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> "From rest + distance + time" is always <span class="eq">a = 2s/t²</span> — substitute once and go: 2(40)/16 = 5 m s⁻². Trap: option <b>(a) 2.5 m s⁻²</b> is precisely what you get by forgetting the ½, so it is planted for the careless; the ½ is the single most-dropped factor in this chapter. Useful ratio to carry: since s ∝ t² from rest, distances in successive equal intervals go 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 (the odd numbers).</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (c) 5 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">An object accelerates uniformly at 10 ms⁻² along a straight track. At t = 0, it is at x = −8 m with velocity 3 ms⁻¹. Find its position (in m) at t = 3 s</td><td class="topic">x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=01qq44qMaHo" target="_blank">Determine the acceleration, the displacement and the distance the particle travels</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">46</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> The question asks for a <b>position</b>, not a displacement, so the starting coordinate matters. The equation s = ut + ½at² gives only the <i>change</i> in position; the actual coordinate is that change added to where the particle began. Since the particle starts at x₀ = −8 m (to the left of the origin), the full position equation must carry that offset. Everything here is signed: positive x is one direction, and x₀, v₀ and a all happen to point the same way except for the negative starting coordinate.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">x = x₀ + v₀·t + ½·a·t²</span> — x = final position (m), x₀ = initial position (m), v₀ = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), a = acceleration (m s⁻²), t = time (s). <span class="why">Units: x₀ = m; v₀t = m s⁻¹ × s = m; ½at² = m s⁻² × s² = m — all three terms are metres, so the sum is a legal position.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> a is constant at 10 m s⁻² and the track is straight, so signed scalars suffice.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with signs and units: x₀ = <b>−8 m</b>, v₀ = <b>3 m s⁻¹</b>, a = <b>10 m s⁻²</b>, t = <b>3 s</b>.<span class="why">Only the position is negative — the particle starts 8 m on the negative side of the origin but moves in the +x direction.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Write the position equation: x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at².</div>
<div class="step">3. Evaluate the drift term: v₀·t = 3 m s⁻¹ × 3 s = <b>+9 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. Evaluate the acceleration term: ½ × 10 m s⁻² × (3 s)² = 5 m s⁻² × 9 s² = <b>+45 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Add all three terms: x = (−8 m) + (9 m) + (45 m) = <b>+46 m</b>.<span class="why">The displacement is 9 + 45 = 54 m; starting 8 m behind the origin leaves the particle at 54 − 8 = 46 m.</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Sanity check: the particle ends on the positive side, as it must — it drifts and accelerates in the +x direction for 3 s, far more than the 8 m deficit it began with. Its velocity at t = 3 s is v = 3 + 10(3) = 33 m s⁻¹.</div>
<pre>
 term          substitution              value
 x₀            given                     −8 m
 v₀·t          (3 m/s)(3 s)              +9 m
 ½·a·t²        ½(10 m/s²)(3 s)²          +45 m
                                        ───────
 x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²                     46 m

  −8        0                                46
 ──*────────|──────────────────────────────>─*──> +x
  start   origin                            t = 3 s
  (displacement = 9 + 45 = 54 m)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Watch the noun: <b>"position" needs x₀; "displacement" does not</b>. Dropping the x₀ term here gives 54 m — the classic near-miss. Write the general form <span class="eq">x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²</span> every time and set x₀ = 0 only when the problem truly starts at the origin. Trap: a negative <i>position</i> does not mean the particle is moving backwards; the sign of x₀ and the sign of v₀ are independent pieces of information.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 46 m.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A train moving at 36 kmh⁻¹ comes to rest in a distance of 200 m after brakes are applied. Calculate the retardation in cm/s²</td><td class="topic">Retardation, unit conversion</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RjHB0pKnhuQ" target="_blank">Brakes are applied to a train travelling at 72 km/h. After passing over 200m, its velocity...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">25</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Two conversions bracket this problem. The speed arrives in <b>km h⁻¹</b> and must become m s⁻¹ before any equation is used; the answer is demanded in <b>cm s⁻²</b> and must be converted <i>out</i> of SI at the very end. In between it is a plain stopping-distance calculation: "comes to rest" fixes v = 0, time is absent, so v² = u² + 2as is the equation of choice. Doing physics in SI and converting only at the boundaries is what keeps this safe.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">1 km h⁻¹ = 5/18 m s⁻¹</span>; <span class="eq">1 m s⁻² = 100 cm s⁻²</span>. Kinematics: <span class="eq">v² = u² + 2·a·s</span> ⟹ <span class="eq">a = (v² − u²) ÷ (2s)</span>, with v, u in m s⁻¹, s in m, a in m s⁻². <span class="why">Units: (m² s⁻²) ÷ (m) = m s⁻² ✓.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the retardation is uniform over the whole 200 m and the track is straight.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Convert the initial speed: u = 36 km h⁻¹ × (5/18) = 180/18 = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">36 km h⁻¹ = 10 m s⁻¹ is worth memorising as an anchor.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Identify the rest of the data: v = <b>0 m s⁻¹</b> ("comes to rest"), s = <b>200 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. Choose the equation without t: v² = u² + 2as, since no time is given or asked.</div>
<div class="step">4. Substitute with units: (0 m s⁻¹)² = (10 m s⁻¹)² + 2 × a × 200 m ⟹ 0 = 100 m² s⁻² + (400 m)·a.</div>
<div class="step">5. Solve in SI: a = −100 m² s⁻² ÷ 400 m = <b>−0.25 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">The minus sign says it opposes the motion — a retardation of magnitude 0.25 m s⁻².</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Convert to the demanded unit: 0.25 m s⁻² × 100 cm/m = <b>25 cm s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">1 m = 100 cm, and only the length unit changes, so multiply by 100.</span></div>
<div class="step">7. Sanity check: stopping time t = u/|a| = 10 ÷ 0.25 = 40 s, and s = ½(10)(40) = <b>200 m</b> ✓ — a long, gentle stop, as expected for a train.</div>
<pre>
 step          working                        result
 u             36 km/h × 5/18                 10 m/s
 v             "comes to rest"                0 m/s
 s             given                          200 m
 a = (v²−u²)/(2s) = (0 − 100)/(2 × 200)       −0.25 m/s²
 |a| in cm/s² = 0.25 × 100                    25 cm/s²
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Do the whole calculation in <b>SI, then convert the answer last</b> — mixing cm and m mid-equation is how marks vanish. Keep <span class="eq">|a| = u²/(2s)</span> ready for every "brakes → rest" item: 100/400 = 0.25 m s⁻² in one step. Trap: the question quietly demands <b>cm s⁻²</b>, so 0.25 is <i>not</i> the answer — a factor-of-100 read-the-question trap, and the most common loss here.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 25 cm s⁻² (retardation; = 0.25 m s⁻²).</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">An object starts with u = 10 m/s and reaches v = 30 m/s in 10 s. Find acceleration. (a) 1 m/s² (b) 2 m/s² (c) 3 m/s² (d) 4 m/s²</td><td class="topic">a = (v−u)/t</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AHdBeRz_T68" target="_blank">Derive v = u + at (equation of motion derivation)</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">b</span>2 m/s²</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Acceleration measures <b>how quickly velocity changes</b>, so it is built from the <i>difference</i> of two velocities divided by the time that difference took. The object's actual speed (10 or 30 m s⁻¹) is irrelevant on its own — only the 20 m s⁻¹ gap and the 10 s window decide the answer. Spread that gap evenly across the window and you have the acceleration.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a = (v − u) ÷ t = Δv ÷ t</span> — u = initial velocity (m s⁻¹), v = final velocity (m s⁻¹), t = time (s), a in <b>m s⁻²</b>. <span class="why">Dividing m s⁻¹ by s gives m s⁻², the SI unit of acceleration, so the formula is dimensionally self-checking.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> uniform acceleration, so this average equals the instantaneous value at every moment.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with units: u = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b>, v = <b>30 m s⁻¹</b>, t = <b>10 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">2. Change in velocity: Δv = 30 m s⁻¹ − 10 m s⁻¹ = <b>20 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">Always final minus initial; a positive Δv means the object is speeding up.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Rate of change: a = Δv ÷ t = 20 m s⁻¹ ÷ 10 s = <b>2 m s⁻²</b> — option <b>(b)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. Read it physically: the object gains 2 m s⁻¹ of velocity in each second, ten times over, for a total gain of 20 m s⁻¹ ✓.</div>
<div class="step">5. Verify with v = u + at: 10 m s⁻¹ + (2 m s⁻²)(10 s) = 10 + 20 = <b>30 m s⁻¹</b> ✓, matching the stated final velocity.</div>
<pre>
 t (s)     0    2    4    6    8    10
 v (m/s)  10   14   18   22   26   30
            +4   +4   +4   +4   +4        → +2 m/s per second

 a = (30 − 10)/10 = 20/10 = 2 m/s²   → (b)
 [trap] v/t = 30/10 = 3 m/s²  → distractor (c)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Read <b>a = Δv/t</b> as "velocity gained per second" and answer in your head. Trap: the distractor <b>(c) 3 m s⁻²</b> is exactly v/t — planted for anyone who forgets to subtract u. Acceleration is never velocity ÷ time unless the body <i>starts from rest</i> (u = 0), which is not the case here.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (b) 2 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">A body moving with constant acceleration covers 20 m in 4 s. If u = 0, find a. (a) 2 m/s² (b) 2.5 m/s² (c) 3 m/s² (d) 3.5 m/s²</td><td class="topic">s = ut + ½at²</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XFcEBISYCVA" target="_blank">Acceleration Calculations - Final Velocity After Any Displacement</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">b</span>2.5 m/s²</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> With u = 0 the displacement equation loses its first term and the distance is controlled by a and t² alone. The given pair (distance, time) therefore pins down the acceleration uniquely. The <b>t²</b> dependence is the physical heart of it: distance piles up faster and faster because the body is moving faster in every successive second.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">s = u·t + ½·a·t²</span> — s (m), u (m s⁻¹), a (m s⁻²), t (s); with u = 0 this is <span class="eq">s = ½·a·t²</span>, so <span class="eq">a = 2s ÷ t²</span>. <span class="why">Check: ½at² has m s⁻² × s² = m, a genuine length; and 2s/t² returns m ÷ s² = m s⁻² ✓.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the acceleration is constant over the full 4 s of straight-line motion.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with units: u = <b>0 m s⁻¹</b> (given), s = <b>20 m</b>, t = <b>4 s</b>; unknown a.</div>
<div class="step">2. Substitute: 20 m = (0 m s⁻¹)(4 s) + ½ × a × (4 s)².</div>
<div class="step">3. First term is zero since u = 0; second term needs (4 s)² = <b>16 s²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. So 20 m = ½ × a × 16 s² = a × <b>8 s²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. a = 20 m ÷ 8 s² = <b>2.5 m s⁻²</b> — option <b>(b)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">6. Verify: ½ × 2.5 m s⁻² × 16 s² = <b>20 m</b> ✓. Also v = at = 2.5 × 4 = 10 m s⁻¹, and ½(0 + 10)(4) = 20 m ✓ by the average-velocity route.</div>
<pre>
 u = 0, s = 20 m, t = 4 s
 20 = ½·a·(4)²  =  8a   ⇒  a = 2.5 m/s²    → (b)

 distance from rest grows as t² :
 t (s)    1     2     3     4
 s (m)   1.25   5   11.25   20
 Δs        1.25  3.75  6.25  8.75   → ratio 1 : 3 : 5 : 7
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> From rest, reach straight for <span class="eq">a = 2s/t²</span> = 2(20)/16 = 2.5 m s⁻². Remember the companion fact shown above: starting from rest, distances covered in successive equal time intervals follow the <b>odd-number ratio 1 : 3 : 5 : 7</b> — it answers a whole family of questions with no algebra. Trap: dropping the ½ gives 1.25 m s⁻²; not squaring t gives 10 m s⁻². Both appear as distractors across this chapter.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (b) 2.5 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 3</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A cyclist accelerates uniformly from rest at A and reaches Y at 9 ms⁻¹, covering the last 10 m of the ride in 2 s. Determine the acceleration (m/s²)</td><td class="topic">Uniform acceleration, last-segment data</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=__XFOhRwn3U" target="_blank">The velocity-time graph shows the motion of a cyclist. Find (i) its acceleration (ii) its velocity a...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">4</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> The data is given for the <b>last 10 m</b> of the ride, not for the whole ride, so the trick is to treat that final stretch as a self-contained sub-problem with its own "initial" velocity. Two facts unlock it: the acceleration is the <i>same</i> everywhere (uniform), and over any constant-acceleration stretch the <b>average velocity equals the mean of the end velocities</b>, ½(u + v). Since we know the stretch's length (10 m), its duration (2 s) and its exit velocity (9 m s⁻¹), the average velocity gives the entry velocity, and the entry-to-exit change then gives a.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> For the last segment: <span class="eq">v_avg = s ÷ t = ½ × (v₁ + v₂)</span>, where v₁ = velocity entering the segment and v₂ = velocity leaving it (both m s⁻¹), s in m, t in s. Then <span class="eq">a = (v₂ − v₁) ÷ t</span> in m s⁻². <span class="why">The mean-of-endpoints rule for average velocity is valid only because a is constant, which makes v(t) linear.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the acceleration is uniform over the whole ride from A to Y, exactly as stated.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data for the <b>last segment</b> only: s = <b>10 m</b>, t = <b>2 s</b>, exit velocity v₂ = <b>9 m s⁻¹</b> (at Y). Unknown: entry velocity v₁ and the acceleration a.</div>
<div class="step">2. Average velocity over that segment: v_avg = s ÷ t = 10 m ÷ 2 s = <b>5 m s⁻¹</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. For constant acceleration, v_avg = ½(v₁ + v₂), so 5 m s⁻¹ = ½ × (v₁ + 9 m s⁻¹).</div>
<div class="step">4. Solve for the entry velocity: v₁ + 9 m s⁻¹ = 10 m s⁻¹ ⟹ v₁ = <b>1 m s⁻¹</b>.<span class="why">The cyclist entered the final 10 m already moving at 1 m s⁻¹.</span></div>
<div class="step">5. Acceleration over the segment: a = (v₂ − v₁) ÷ t = (9 m s⁻¹ − 1 m s⁻¹) ÷ 2 s = 8 m s⁻¹ ÷ 2 s = <b>4 m s⁻²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">6. Check the segment with s = v₁t + ½at²: (1)(2) + ½(4)(2)² = 2 m + 8 m = <b>10 m</b> ✓.</div>
<div class="step">7. Consistency with "starts from rest at A": reaching v₁ = 1 m s⁻¹ from rest at a = 4 m s⁻² takes 0.25 s and covers only 1²/(2×4) = <b>0.125 m</b>, so the whole ride A→Y is 10.125 m — the last 10 m really is almost the entire ride ✓ no contradiction.</div>
<pre>
        |&lt;------------ A to Y (10.125 m) ------------>|
   A    |&lt;-0.125 m->|&lt;--------- last 10 m ----------->| Y
   *====*===========*================================>*
  v=0            v₁ = 1 m/s        (2 s)          v₂ = 9 m/s

  v_avg(last 10 m) = 10/2 = 5 m/s = ½(v₁ + 9)  ⇒ v₁ = 1 m/s
  a = (9 − 1)/2 = 4 m/s²
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> When a problem hands you a <b>partial stretch</b>, apply the equations <i>to that stretch alone</i> — its entry velocity plays the role of u. And whenever distance and time are known over a constant-a stretch, <span class="eq">s/t = ½(u+v)</span> hands you the missing end velocity instantly. Trap: do <b>not</b> use the ride's overall "from rest" u = 0 with s = 10 m and t = 2 s (that would give a = 5 m s⁻² and a final speed of 10 m s⁻¹, contradicting the stated 9 m s⁻¹) — the 10 m is the <i>last</i> part of the ride, not the whole of it.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: 4 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">A particle experiences constant acceleration for 20 s after starting from rest, travelling s₁ in the first 10 s and s₂ in the next 10 s: (a) s₂ = s₁ (b) s₂ = 2s₁ (c) s₂ = 3s₁ (d) s₂ = 4s₁</td><td class="topic">Distance ratio, successive intervals</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-n_RHe44tDo" target="_blank">A particle experiences a constant acceleration for 20 sec after starting from rest...</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mZoMp8vXKnk" target="_blank">A particle experiences constant acceleration for 20 sec after starting from rest. It travels a...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>s₂ = 3s₁</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A proton in a cyclotron changes velocity from 30 km/sec north to 40 km/sec east in 20 sec. Magnitude of average acceleration? (a) 2.5 km/sec² (b) 12.5 km/sec² (c) 22.5 km/sec² (d) 32.5 km/sec²</td><td class="topic">Vector (perpendicular) average acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Da9B_eQPS8Y" target="_blank">A proton in a cyclotron changes its velocity from 30 kms⁻¹...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>2.5 km/sec²</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">A particle starts from rest with constant acceleration for 6 s: distance d₁ in first 2 s, d₂ in next 2 s, d₃ in last 2 s: (a) d₁:d₂:d₃ = 1:1:1 (b) 1:2:3 (c) 1:3:5 (d) 1:5:9</td><td class="topic">Odd-number ratio of successive distances</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aq9va5tGxas" target="_blank">A particle accelerates for 6 seconds. Find the ratio d1:d2:d3: Physics Numerical (Kinematics)</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>d₁ : d₂ : d₃ = 1 : 3 : 5</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">*(Multiple correct)* A car accelerates uniformly passing two checkpoints 30 m apart in 4.0 s; speed at the first checkpoint is 5.0 ms⁻¹. (a) velocity at 2nd checkpoint is 10 ms⁻¹ (b) velocity at 2nd checkpoint is 20 ms⁻¹ (c) acceleration is 2.5 ms⁻² (d) acceleration is 1.25 ms⁻²</td><td class="topic">s = ut + ½at², multi-correct</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v_qTkpGFH2E" target="_blank">A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly for 10 seconds to a velocity of 8...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">ad</span>the velocity of car at second check point is 10 ms⁻¹ · the acceleration of car is 1.25 ms⁻²</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A multiple-correct item that is really one calculation checked twice. The car does <b>not</b> start from rest — it is already doing 5.0 m s⁻¹ at the first checkpoint — so the ut term survives and s = ut + ½at² must be used in full. Once a is found from the 30 m / 4.0 s data, the velocity at the second checkpoint follows from v = u + at, and both results are compared against the four options.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">s = u·t + ½·a·t²</span> — s = 30 m between checkpoints, u = 5.0 m s⁻¹, t = 4.0 s, a in m s⁻². Then <span class="eq">v = u + a·t</span> in m s⁻¹. <span class="why">Both terms of s are lengths: ut = m s⁻¹ × s = m and ½at² = m s⁻² × s² = m ✓.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the acceleration is uniform between the two checkpoints ("accelerating uniformly") and the road is straight.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data with units: u = <b>5.0 m s⁻¹</b> (first checkpoint), s = <b>30 m</b>, t = <b>4.0 s</b>; find a and v.</div>
<div class="step">2. Substitute into s = ut + ½at²: 30 m = (5.0 m s⁻¹)(4.0 s) + ½ × a × (4.0 s)².</div>
<div class="step">3. Evaluate the known term: (5.0 m s⁻¹)(4.0 s) = <b>20 m</b>. And (4.0 s)² = 16 s², so ½ × a × 16 s² = a × <b>8 s²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. So 30 m = 20 m + (8 s²)·a ⟹ (8 s²)·a = <b>10 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. a = 10 m ÷ 8 s² = <b>1.25 m s⁻²</b> ⟹ option <b>(d)</b> is correct, and (c) 2.5 m s⁻² is wrong.</div>
<div class="step">6. Velocity at the second checkpoint: v = u + at = 5.0 m s⁻¹ + (1.25 m s⁻²)(4.0 s) = 5.0 + 5.0 = <b>10 m s⁻¹</b> ⟹ option <b>(a)</b> is correct, and (b) 20 m s⁻¹ is wrong.</div>
<div class="step">7. Cross-check both at once: v_avg = ½(u+v) = ½(5.0 + 10) = 7.5 m s⁻¹, and 7.5 m s⁻¹ × 4.0 s = <b>30 m</b> ✓ — matches the given separation exactly.</div>
<pre>
   CP1                30 m, 4.0 s                CP2
    *=====================================>========*
  u = 5.0 m/s        a = 1.25 m/s²           v = 10 m/s

 option                              verdict
 (a) v at 2nd checkpoint = 10 m/s    ✓ correct
 (b) v at 2nd checkpoint = 20 m/s    ✗
 (c) a = 2.5 m/s²                    ✗ (that is 2s/t², ignoring u)
 (d) a = 1.25 m/s²                   ✓ correct
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> In multi-correct items the wrong options are usually the <i>right</i> arithmetic done on a <b>wrong assumption</b> — here (c) 2.5 m s⁻² is exactly 2s/t², i.e. what you get by pretending the car started from rest. Whenever u ≠ 0, the ut term must stay. Fastest check: <span class="eq">s/t = ½(u+v)</span> ⟹ 30/4 = 7.5 = ½(5 + v) ⟹ v = 10 m s⁻¹ settles (a) and (b) before you ever compute a.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (a) velocity at 2nd checkpoint = 10 m s⁻¹ and (d) acceleration = 1.25 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 4</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A small body starts from rest on a frictionless inclined plane. If Sₙ is the distance travelled from t = n−1 to t = n, the ratio Sₙ : Sₙ₊₁ is: (a) (2n−1)/2n (b) (2n+1)/(2n−1) (c) (2n−1)/(2n+1) (d) 2n/(2n+1)</td><td class="topic">Distance-in-nth-second ratio</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MPJA_t3pjCM" target="_blank">A body moves on a frictionless plane starting from rest. If Sn is distance moved between t = n – 1</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>(2n − 1)/(2n + 1)</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">A car accelerates from rest at 2 m/s² for some time, then retards at 4 m/s² and comes to rest. Maximum speed attained if it remains in motion for 3 s? (a) 2 m/s (b) 3 m/s (c) 4 m/s (d) 6 m/s</td><td class="topic">Two-phase accel/decel, max speed</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQIrs080KLs" target="_blank">A car acceleration from rest at a constant rate of 2m/s² for some time. Then, it retards at a constant rate of 4 m/s² and comes to rest</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>4 m/s</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A car starts from rest, accelerates at 5 m/s² for some time, moves at constant speed, then retards at the same rate and stops. Total time 25 s, average speed 20 m/s. How long at constant speed? (a) 10 s (b) 12 s (c) 15 s (d) 18 s</td><td class="topic">Three-phase motion, average speed</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kLuRV7re1oo" target="_blank">A car, starting from rest, accelerates at the rate f through a distance S, then continues at constant speed for time t and then decelerates at the rate f/2</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>15 s</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A three-phase journey: speed up, cruise, slow down. Two structural facts make it tractable. First, the acceleration and the retardation have the <b>same magnitude</b> (5 m s⁻²) and both connect rest to the same peak speed, so the two ramps take <b>equal times</b> and are mirror images. Second, "average speed 20 m s⁻¹ over 25 s" is really a statement about the <b>total distance</b> (= 500 m). Writing distance and time in terms of the ramp time t₁ then closes the problem with a quadratic. Reading it as a v–t trapezium makes every term visible.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> Ramp: <span class="eq">v = a·t₁</span> and <span class="eq">s_ramp = ½·a·t₁²</span> (m). Cruise: <span class="eq">s_cruise = v·t₂</span> (m). Totals: <span class="eq">t₁ + t₂ + t₃ = 25 s</span> and <span class="eq">s_total = v_avg × t_total</span>. <span class="why">Average speed × total time returns the total path length: m s⁻¹ × s = m ✓.</span> <b>Assumptions:</b> both ramps are uniform at 5 m s⁻², the cruise is at exactly the peak speed, and motion is in one straight line (so distance = displacement).</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Total distance from the average speed: s_total = 20 m s⁻¹ × 25 s = <b>500 m</b>.</div>
<div class="step">2. Let t₁ = accelerating time, t₂ = constant-speed time, t₃ = decelerating time (all s). Peak speed v = a·t₁ = <b>5t₁</b> m s⁻¹.</div>
<div class="step">3. Since the retardation has the same magnitude (5 m s⁻²) and brings v back to rest, t₃ = v ÷ 5 = t₁.<span class="why">Same rate, same speed change ⇒ same duration — the v–t graph is a symmetric trapezium.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Time budget: t₁ + t₂ + t₃ = 25 s ⟹ 2t₁ + t₂ = 25 s ⟹ <b>t₂ = 25 − 2t₁</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Distances: ramp up = ½(5)t₁² = 2.5t₁² m; ramp down = 2.5t₁² m (identical); cruise = v·t₂ = 5t₁·t₂ m.</div>
<div class="step">6. Sum to 500 m: 2.5t₁² + 2.5t₁² + 5t₁t₂ = 500 ⟹ <b>5t₁² + 5t₁t₂ = 500</b> ⟹ t₁² + t₁t₂ = 100.</div>
<div class="step">7. Substitute t₂ = 25 − 2t₁: t₁² + t₁(25 − 2t₁) = 100 ⟹ t₁² + 25t₁ − 2t₁² = 100 ⟹ <b>−t₁² + 25t₁ = 100</b>.</div>
<div class="step">8. Rearrange: t₁² − 25t₁ + 100 = 0 ⟹ t₁ = [25 ± √(625 − 400)]/2 = [25 ± 15]/2 = <b>20 s or 5 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">9. Reject t₁ = 20 s: it needs 2t₁ = 40 s of ramps inside a 25 s journey — impossible. So <b>t₁ = 5 s</b>.<span class="why">Physical admissibility, not algebra, picks the root.</span></div>
<div class="step">10. Therefore t₂ = 25 − 2(5) = <b>15 s</b> — option <b>(c)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">11. Verify: v = 5 × 5 = 25 m s⁻¹; ramps 2 × ½(5)(5²) = 125 m; cruise 25 × 15 = 375 m; total 125 + 375 = <b>500 m</b> ✓ and 500 ÷ 25 = 20 m s⁻¹ ✓.</div>
<pre>
 v (m/s)
  25 |      *--------------------*          v = 5t₁ = 25 m/s
     |     /|                    |\
     |    / |      cruise        | \
     |   /  |      t₂ = 15 s     |  \
   0 |__/___|____________________|___\____ t (s)
       |t₁=5|                    |t₃=5|
       |&lt;---------- 25 s -------------->|

 area = ½(t₂ + 25)(25) = ½(15 + 25)(25) = 500 m  ✓ (trapezium)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Draw the <b>v–t trapezium</b> and read the area with <span class="eq">s = ½ × (sum of parallel sides) × height = ½(t₂ + t_total)·v</span> — here ½(t₂+25)(5t₁) = 500 gets you there in one line, no quadratic in sight. Remember: <b>equal accelerating and retarding rates ⇒ equal ramp times</b> (symmetric trapezium). Trap: quadratics in kinematics throw two roots, and the unphysical one (t₁ = 20 s here) is not "also correct" — always test it against the total time.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (c) 15 s.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">A particle starts from the origin with velocity 10 m/s, constant acceleration until velocity reaches 50 m/s; acceleration then reversed. Find the velocity on return to the starting point: (a) Zero (b) 10 m/s (c) 50 m/s (d) 70 m/s</td><td class="topic">Reversed acceleration, return velocity</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0uiStyjdRrc" target="_blank">A particle starts from the origin with a velocity 10 m/s and moves with a constant acceleration...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>70 m/s</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">A body moves for 9 s total, starting from rest with uniform acceleration then uniform retardation (twice the acceleration). Time during retardation: (a) 3 s (b) 4.5 s (c) 5 s (d) 6 s</td><td class="topic">Accel + retardation, total time</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pXHc8hfUupE" target="_blank">A body moves for a total of nine sec. starting from rest with uniform acceleration and then</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>3 s</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">6</td><td class="q">A body with uniform acceleration crosses 65 m in the 5th second and 105 m in the 9th second. How far will it go in 20 s? (a) 2040 m (b) 240 m (c) 2400 m (d) 2004 m</td><td class="topic">Distance in nth second → total distance</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Wjh00dzPZA" target="_blank">A body moving with a uniform acceleration crosses a distance of 65 m in the 5th second and 105...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>2400 m</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">7</td><td class="q">Acceleration of a particle starting from rest varies as a = αt + β. Find the velocity after time t. (a) βt²/2 + βt (b) αt²/2 + αt (c) βt²/2 + αt (d) αt²/2 + βt</td><td class="topic">Integrate a(t) to get v(t)</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UqOx_iIm5oQ" target="_blank">The acceleration a of a particle starting from rest varies with time according to relation a = αt + β</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>αt²/2 + βt</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">8</td><td class="q">A body starts moving with v₀ = 10 ms⁻¹, retardation = 0.2v². Find velocity after 2 s</td><td class="topic">Velocity-dependent retardation, separable ODE</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UM2RkwPZ4bk" target="_blank">A body starts moving with a velocity v0 = 10 ms⁻¹. It experiences a retardation equal to 0.2v...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>+2 ms⁻¹</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">9</td><td class="q">A body has initial velocity 3 m/s and acceleration 1 m/sec² normal to the initial velocity. Velocity 4 s after start: (a) 7 m/sec along initial velocity (b) 7 m/sec along the normal (c) 7 m/sec midway (d) 5 m/sec at tan⁻¹(4/3) to initial velocity</td><td class="topic">Perpendicular acceleration, resultant velocity</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrK08m41jjU" target="_blank">A body has an initial velocity of 3 m/s and has an acceleration of 1 m/sec²...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>5 m/sec at an angle of tan⁻¹(4/3) with the direction of initial velocity</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">10</td><td class="q">A person on an escalator takes t₁ to reach the top when it is moving, and t₂ when it is standing. How long if he walks up the moving escalator? (a) t₂−t₁ (b) t₁+t₂ (c) t₁t₂/(t₁−t₂) (d) t₁t₂/(t₁+t₂)</td><td class="topic">Relative-speed escalator problem</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Yc9JIDqRUkM" target="_blank">A person takes T1 second on the stationary escalator to cover some distance. The person takes T2...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>t₁t₂/(t₁ + t₂)</span></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 5</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A car starts from rest and moves on straight line with constant acceleration a₀. After time t₀, brakes is applied which cause of deceleration of magnitude as initial acceleration. Find the distance travelled by car is given by n·a₀·t₀². Find n. [wording exactly as printed]</td><td class="topic">Two-phase motion, distance coefficient</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=izb3QsWjTKI" target="_blank">A car starts from rest and moves with uniform acceleration a on a straight road from time t=0...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval flagged">1 <span class="warn" title="Book wording defect — preserved verbatim above, not tidied. Verified against the PDF (p.155) at 12x, the book prints: &lsquo;After time t₀, brakes is applied which cause of deceleration of magnitude as initial acceleration. Find the distance travelled by car is given by na₀t₀². Find n.&rsquo; The mangled clause is the book&rsquo;s own; its intended meaning is that the braking deceleration equals the initial acceleration a₀. Read that way the question is well posed and the key&rsquo;s n = 1 is correct: ½a₀t₀² while speeding up + ½a₀t₀² while braking = a₀t₀².">⚠</span></span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> The book's clause &mdash; &ldquo;brakes is applied which cause of deceleration of magnitude as initial acceleration&rdquo; &mdash; is ungrammatical but unambiguous once parsed: the brakes produce a <b>deceleration of the same magnitude as the initial acceleration</b>, i.e. a₀. That symmetry is the whole problem. The car ramps up from rest to a peak speed in time t₀, then ramps back down to rest at the identical rate — so the second phase is the exact mirror of the first, takes the same time t₀, and covers the same distance. The total is therefore just <i>twice</i> the first phase, and n falls out with no quadratic anywhere.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> Phase 1 (rest → peak): <span class="eq">v = a₀·t₀</span> (m s⁻¹) and <span class="eq">s₁ = ½·a₀·t₀²</span> (m). Phase 2 (peak → rest at rate a₀): <span class="eq">s₂ = v² ÷ (2a₀)</span> (m). Total <span class="eq">s = s₁ + s₂ ≡ n·a₀·t₀²</span>. <span class="why">Dimensional check on the target form: a₀t₀² has m s⁻² × s² = m, so n must be a pure dimensionless number — as a coefficient should be.</span> <b>Assumptions:</b> both rates are uniform, the brakes are applied exactly at t₀, and the car travels in one straight line so distance = displacement.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Phase 1 — accelerate from rest at a₀ for time t₀. Peak speed: v = u + a₀t₀ = 0 + a₀t₀ = <b>a₀t₀</b> (m s⁻¹).</div>
<div class="step">2. Distance in phase 1: s₁ = ut₀ + ½a₀t₀² = 0 + ½a₀t₀² = <b>½·a₀·t₀²</b> (m).</div>
<div class="step">3. Phase 2 — brakes give a deceleration of magnitude a₀ (same as the initial acceleration), acting until the car stops (final velocity 0).</div>
<div class="step">4. Distance in phase 2, using v² = u² + 2as with u = a₀t₀, v = 0, a = −a₀: 0 = (a₀t₀)² − 2a₀·s₂.</div>
<div class="step">5. So s₂ = (a₀t₀)² ÷ (2a₀) = (a₀²t₀²) ÷ (2a₀) = <b>½·a₀·t₀²</b> (m) — identical to s₁, confirming the mirror symmetry.<span class="why">Same rate + same speed range ⇒ same distance and same duration t₀.</span></div>
<div class="step">6. Total distance: s = s₁ + s₂ = ½a₀t₀² + ½a₀t₀² = <b>a₀·t₀²</b> (m).</div>
<div class="step">7. Compare with the given form s = n·a₀·t₀²: a₀t₀² = n·a₀t₀² ⟹ <b>n = 1</b>.</div>
<div class="step">8. Check by area: the v–t graph is a triangle of base 2t₀ and height a₀t₀, so s = ½ × 2t₀ × a₀t₀ = <b>a₀t₀²</b> ✓ — same answer in one line.</div>
<pre>
 v
 a₀t₀ |        *                peak speed = a₀t₀
      |       / \
      |      /   \              both slopes have magnitude a₀
      |     /     \
    0 |____/_______\_______ t
        0   t₀    2t₀

 s = area of triangle = ½ × (2t₀) × (a₀t₀) = a₀t₀²
 given s = n·a₀t₀²   ⇒   n = 1
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> When acceleration and deceleration have <b>equal magnitudes</b> and the motion runs rest → peak → rest, the v–t graph is an <b>isoceles triangle</b>: equal times, equal distances, and total distance = ½ × base × height in a single stroke. Trap: answering n = ½ by stopping after phase 1 — the question asks for the distance travelled by the car over the <i>whole</i> journey, braking included.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: n = 1 (total distance = a₀t₀²).</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">A particle with uniform acceleration travels distances S and S′ in two successive n-second intervals. If S′ = 16, S = 7, n = 3, find a</td><td class="topic">a = (S′−S)/n²</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IIPUNkNj3_4" target="_blank">A particle moving with a uniform acceleration along a straight line covers distances a and b in successive intervals of p and q seconds</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">1</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Under constant acceleration the distance covered in each successive equal interval keeps growing, and the <b>growth per interval is itself constant</b>. That surplus, S′ − S, is exactly the extra ground gained because the particle is faster in the second window than in the first — and it works out to a·n², independent of the (unknown) starting velocity. That independence is what makes the result so useful: two distances and one interval length determine a, with u never appearing.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">S′ − S = a·n²</span> ⟹ <span class="eq">a = (S′ − S) ÷ n²</span> — S = distance in the first n-second interval (m), S′ = distance in the next n-second interval (m), n = length of each interval (s), a = uniform acceleration (m s⁻²). <span class="why">Units: m ÷ s² = m s⁻² ✓.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> the two intervals are equal in length, consecutive, and the acceleration is uniform throughout.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Derive the relation first. Let u be the velocity at the start of the first interval. Distance in the first n seconds: S = u·n + ½·a·n² (m).</div>
<div class="step">2. Distance covered in the first <b>2n</b> seconds: s(2n) = u(2n) + ½a(2n)² = 2un + 2an² (m).</div>
<div class="step">3. Distance in the <i>second</i> interval alone: S′ = s(2n) − S = (2un + 2an²) − (un + ½an²) = <b>u·n + (3/2)·a·n²</b> (m).</div>
<div class="step">4. Subtract: S′ − S = [un + 1.5an²] − [un + 0.5an²] = <b>a·n²</b>.<span class="why">The un terms cancel — the surplus does not depend on how fast the particle was already going.</span></div>
<div class="step">5. Substitute the data with units: S′ = <b>16 m</b>, S = <b>7 m</b>, n = <b>3 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">6. a = (S′ − S) ÷ n² = (16 m − 7 m) ÷ (3 s)² = 9 m ÷ 9 s² = <b>1 m s⁻²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">7. Consistency check — recover u from S = un + ½an²: 7 = 3u + ½(1)(9) = 3u + 4.5 ⟹ u = <b>0.833 m s⁻¹</b>. Then S′ = un + 1.5an² = 2.5 + 6.75... let us verify precisely: S′ = 3(0.8333) + 1.5(1)(9) = 2.5 + 13.5 = <b>16 m</b> ✓ — matches the given S′.</div>
<pre>
 |&lt;--- n = 3 s --->|&lt;--- n = 3 s --->|
 |----- S = 7 m ---|---- S' = 16 m ---|
 t=0              t=3 s             t=6 s

 S  = un + ½an²
 S' = un + (3/2)an²
 ─────────────────────  subtract
 S' − S = a n²    ⇒   a = (16 − 7)/3² = 9/9 = 1 m/s²
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Commit <span class="eq">a = (S′ − S)/n²</span> to memory — it answers every "distances in two successive equal intervals" question in one division and <b>never needs u</b>. The general version for <i>unequal</i> intervals p and q is worth carrying too. Trap: n is the <b>length of each interval in seconds (3 s), not the interval number</b>; and S′ − S must be the <i>second minus the first</i>, or the sign of a flips.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: a = 1 m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">An engine can produce a maximum acceleration of 4 ms⁻²; brakes give maximum retardation of 6 ms⁻². Minimum time to cover 3 km: (a) 30 s (b) 40 s (c) 50 s (d) 60 s</td><td class="topic">Optimal accel/decel, minimum time</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y6jciAYQWoA" target="_blank">The engine of a vehicle can produce a maximum acceleration of 4 ms⁻²</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>50 s</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">A car accelerates at rate f through distance S, continues at constant speed for time t, decelerates at f/2 to rest. Total distance 15S; find S</td><td class="topic">Three-phase motion, distance</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kLuRV7re1oo" target="_blank">A car starting from rest, accelerates at the rate f through a distance s, then continues at constant speed for time t and then decelerates at the rate f/2</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>S = (1/72) ft²</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">If retardation from air resistance is one-tenth of g, find the % change in time to return from maximum height: (a) increases 9% (b) decreases 9% (c) increases 11% (d) decreases 11%</td><td class="topic">Effective g with air resistance</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L_yu6bUYpvY" target="_blank">The friction of the air causes a vertical retardation equal to 10% of the acceleration due to gravity</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>increases by 11%</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Air resistance always <b>opposes the motion</b>, so it flips direction at the top of the flight — and that is the entire physics of this problem. Going <i>up</i>, drag acts downward, adding to gravity: the effective deceleration is g + g/10 = <b>1.1g</b>. Coming <i>down</i>, drag acts upward, opposing gravity: the effective acceleration is only g − g/10 = <b>0.9g</b>. The ball is therefore braked hard on the way up but accelerated feebly on the way down, so the <b>descent takes longer than the ascent</b> — with drag, the up-down symmetry of free fall is broken.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> Upward phase (u → 0 at rate 1.1g): <span class="eq">t_up = u ÷ (1.1g)</span> and <span class="eq">H = u² ÷ (2 × 1.1g)</span>. Downward phase (rest → ground over height H at rate 0.9g): <span class="eq">t_down = √(2H ÷ (0.9g))</span>. All times in <b>s</b>, H in m, g in m s⁻². <span class="why">√(m ÷ m s⁻²) = √(s²) = s ✓.</span> <b>Assumptions:</b> the retardation from air is a <i>constant</i> g/10 (the book's idealisation — real drag depends on speed), the particle is projected vertically, and it returns to the same launch level. <b>Reading:</b> "time to return from the maximum height" is compared against the time it took to <i>reach</i> that height (the descent-vs-ascent comparison) — the standard reading of this item.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Set the two effective accelerations. Going up: a_up = g + 0.1g = <b>1.1g</b> (gravity and drag both act downward, opposing the upward motion).</div>
<div class="step">2. Coming down: a_down = g − 0.1g = <b>0.9g</b> (drag now acts upward, against the downward motion).<span class="why">The sign of the drag term flips with the direction of travel — this asymmetry is the whole question.</span></div>
<div class="step">3. Ascent time from launch speed u to rest: t_up = u ÷ 1.1g = <b>0.909 u/g</b> (s).</div>
<div class="step">4. Maximum height reached: H = u² ÷ (2 × 1.1g) = <b>u² ÷ (2.2g)</b> (m).</div>
<div class="step">5. Descent from rest through that same H at 0.9g: H = ½ × (0.9g) × t_down² ⟹ t_down = √(2H ÷ 0.9g).</div>
<div class="step">6. Substitute H: t_down = √[2 × u²/(2.2g) ÷ (0.9g)] = √[u² ÷ (1.1 × 0.9 × g²)] = √[u² ÷ (0.99 g²)].</div>
<div class="step">7. So t_down = u ÷ (g√0.99) = u ÷ (0.99499 g) = <b>1.00504 u/g</b> (s).</div>
<div class="step">8. Take the ratio: t_down ÷ t_up = (1.00504 u/g) ÷ (0.909 u/g) = 1.00504 × 1.1 = <b>1.1055</b>.<span class="why">Exactly: t_down/t_up = 1.1/√0.99 — the u and g cancel, so the result is a pure number independent of how hard it was thrown.</span></div>
<div class="step">9. Percentage change: (1.1055 − 1) × 100 = <b>+10.55% ≈ 11% increase</b> — option <b>(c)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">10. Sanity check on the sign: the descent is driven by only 0.9g while the ascent was braked by 1.1g, so the return must take <i>longer</i> — an increase, ruling out (b) and (d) immediately.</div>
<pre>
                   ▲ top (v = 0)
                  / \
     up: a = 1.1g/   \  down: a = 0.9g
   (gravity+drag)/     \ (gravity−drag)
                *       ▼
              launch  return

 t_up   = u/(1.1g)          = 0.909  u/g
 H      = u²/(2·1.1g)       = u²/(2.2g)
 t_down = √(2H/0.9g)        = u/(g√0.99) = 1.005 u/g

 t_down/t_up = 1.1/√0.99 = 1.1055  →  +10.55% ≈ 11%   → (c)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Fold air resistance into an <b>effective g</b> and the problem becomes ordinary kinematics: <b>g_up = g(1+k), g_down = g(1−k)</b> for a drag of k·g. Carry the three standard results for k = 0.1: height <b>decreases ~9%</b>, ascent time <b>decreases ~9%</b>, and descent time is <b>~11% longer than ascent</b> — this lesson asks two of the three (see also Level 5 Q7). Trap: with drag, <b>t_up ≠ t_down</b> — the free-fall habit of assuming a symmetric flight is exactly what the question punishes; the way down always takes longer.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (c) increases by 11%.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">6</td><td class="q">A particle moves along parabolic path x = y² + 2y + 2 such that the Y-component of velocity stays 5 ms⁻¹. Find the magnitude of acceleration</td><td class="topic">Parametric path, constant vy</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D97JI8vEk0s" target="_blank">A particle moves along the parabolic path x = y² + 2y + 2 in such a way that the Y-component of...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>50 ms⁻²</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">7</td><td class="q">Air friction causes vertical retardation equal to 10% of g (g = 10 ms⁻²). The maximum height will be decreased by: (a) 8% (b) 9% (c) 10% (d) 11%</td><td class="topic">Effective g, % decrease in height</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L_yu6bUYpvY" target="_blank">The friction of the air causes a vertical retardation equal to 10% of the acceleration due to gravity</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gsx8VyMwLMY" target="_blank">The friction of the air causes a vertical retardation equal to 10% of the acceleration due to grav</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">b</span>9%</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">8</td><td class="q">A particle moves along space curve x = e⁻ᵗcos t, y = e⁻ᵗsin t, z = e⁻ᵗ. Find the magnitude of (i) velocity and (ii) acceleration at any time t</td><td class="topic">Vector differentiation of a space curve</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yq4Cj1_bmnE" target="_blank">Calculus 3 Lecture 12.4: Velocity and Acceleration of Vector Functions</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">(i) √3 e⁻ᵗ  (ii) √5 e⁻ᵗ</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A space curve given as r(t) = x(t)î + y(t)ĵ + z(t)k̂ is handled by <b>differentiating each component separately</b> — velocity is dr/dt, acceleration is d²r/dt², and magnitudes come from the 3-D Pythagoras. The only real work is the product rule on e⁻ᵗcos t and e⁻ᵗsin t, and then the identity <b>sin²t + cos²t = 1</b>, which sweeps all the trigonometry away and leaves a clean answer. Physically this is a spiral decaying towards the origin: both the speed and the acceleration die off like e⁻ᵗ.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">v = dr/dt</span>, <span class="eq">a = dv/dt</span>; magnitudes <span class="eq">|v| = √(vₓ² + v_y² + v_z²)</span> and <span class="eq">|a| = √(aₓ² + a_y² + a_z²)</span>, in m s⁻¹ and m s⁻² when x, y, z are in m and t in s. Product rule: <span class="eq">d/dt(e⁻ᵗ f) = e⁻ᵗ(f′ − f)</span>. <span class="why">Each component is differentiated independently because î, ĵ, k̂ are fixed directions — a vector derivative is just three scalar derivatives.</span></div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Given x = e⁻ᵗcos t, y = e⁻ᵗsin t, z = e⁻ᵗ. Differentiate x by the product rule: vₓ = −e⁻ᵗcos t − e⁻ᵗsin t = <b>−e⁻ᵗ(cos t + sin t)</b>.</div>
<div class="step">2. Similarly for y: v_y = −e⁻ᵗsin t + e⁻ᵗcos t = <b>e⁻ᵗ(cos t − sin t)</b>. And v_z = d/dt(e⁻ᵗ) = <b>−e⁻ᵗ</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. Square and add: |v|² = e⁻²ᵗ[(cos t + sin t)² + (cos t − sin t)² + 1].</div>
<div class="step">4. Expand the brackets: (cos t + sin t)² = cos²t + 2 sin t cos t + sin²t = <b>1 + 2 sin t cos t</b>; (cos t − sin t)² = <b>1 − 2 sin t cos t</b>.<span class="why">The cross terms are equal and opposite, so they cancel on addition — the reason the answer is so tidy.</span></div>
<div class="step">5. Sum inside: (1 + 2sc) + (1 − 2sc) + 1 = <b>3</b>. Hence |v|² = 3e⁻²ᵗ ⟹ <b>|v| = √3·e⁻ᵗ</b> (m s⁻¹).</div>
<div class="step">6. Now differentiate the velocity components. aₓ = d/dt[−e⁻ᵗ(cos t + sin t)] = e⁻ᵗ(cos t + sin t) − e⁻ᵗ(cos t − sin t) = <b>2e⁻ᵗ sin t</b>.</div>
<div class="step">7. a_y = d/dt[e⁻ᵗ(cos t − sin t)] = −e⁻ᵗ(cos t − sin t) + e⁻ᵗ(−sin t − cos t) = <b>−2e⁻ᵗ cos t</b>. And a_z = d/dt(−e⁻ᵗ) = <b>e⁻ᵗ</b>.</div>
<div class="step">8. Square and add: |a|² = e⁻²ᵗ[4sin²t + 4cos²t + 1] = e⁻²ᵗ[4(sin²t + cos²t) + 1] = e⁻²ᵗ[4 + 1] = <b>5e⁻²ᵗ</b>.</div>
<div class="step">9. Therefore <b>|a| = √5·e⁻ᵗ</b> (m s⁻²).</div>
<div class="step">10. Sanity check at t = 0: |v| = √3 ≈ 1.73 m s⁻¹ and |a| = √5 ≈ 2.24 m s⁻²; both decay as e⁻ᵗ, consistent with a spiral collapsing onto the origin (r → 0 as t → ∞).</div>
<pre>
 component   position       velocity                  acceleration
   x       e⁻ᵗcos t      −e⁻ᵗ(cos t + sin t)         2e⁻ᵗ sin t
   y       e⁻ᵗsin t       e⁻ᵗ(cos t − sin t)        −2e⁻ᵗ cos t
   z       e⁻ᵗ           −e⁻ᵗ                        e⁻ᵗ

 |v|² = e⁻²ᵗ[(1+2sc) + (1−2sc) + 1] = 3e⁻²ᵗ  ⇒ |v| = √3 e⁻ᵗ
 |a|² = e⁻²ᵗ[4sin²t + 4cos²t + 1]   = 5e⁻²ᵗ  ⇒ |a| = √5 e⁻ᵗ
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> For any e⁻ᵗ(trig) curve, use the shortcut <span class="eq">d/dt(e⁻ᵗf) = e⁻ᵗ(f′ − f)</span> and <b>never expand the trigonometry until the final squaring</b> — the cross terms 2 sin t cos t always cancel between the x and y components, leaving pure numbers via sin²+cos² = 1. Trap: |v| is <b>not</b> the derivative of |r| — you must differentiate the components <i>first</i>, then take the magnitude. (Here |r| = √2·e⁻ᵗ, whose derivative √2·e⁻ᵗ is <i>not</i> the speed √3·e⁻ᵗ.)</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (i) |v| = √3·e⁻ᵗ m s⁻¹ ; (ii) |a| = √5·e⁻ᵗ m s⁻².</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">9</td><td class="q">A body starts from rest at A with uniform acceleration a. T seconds later, a second body starts from A with uniform velocity V. Prove the second body is ahead of the first for time (2/a)√(V(V−2aT))</td><td class="topic">Relative-motion proof</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8ouw9FoThiM" target="_blank">A body starts from rest at A and moves with uniform acceleration a in a straight line. T seconds</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="na" title="Descriptive 'prove that…' question, and the answer key prints no entry for Q9 — its list jumps 8 → 10. Coverage gap in the key.">—</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">10</td><td class="q">*(Multiple correct)* A particle is projected in the XY-plane with u = (2î+3ĵ) ms⁻¹ at t = 0 from the origin; acceleration a = î+4ĵ. (a) path is parabolic (b) path is a straight line (c) position at t=1s is (2.5î+5ĵ) m (d) speed at t=1s is √58 ms⁻¹</td><td class="topic">Vector kinematics, multi-correct</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P3u2ZMLvTKU" target="_blank">The velocity of a projectile at the initial point A is (2i+3j) m/s. It's velocity (in m/s) at point</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">acd</span>its path is parabolic · its position at t = 1s is (2.5î + 5ĵ)m · its speed at t = 1 s is √58 ms⁻¹</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Two-dimensional motion with constant acceleration is just the <b>vector form of the same equations of motion</b> — each component evolves independently. The shape of the path is decided by one test: if <b>a is parallel to u</b>, the acceleration only speeds the particle up or slows it down along its existing line, so the path is <b>straight</b>; if a has any component <i>across</i> u, the particle is steered sideways and the path <b>curves into a parabola</b> (constant a always gives a parabola, exactly as in projectile motion where a = −gĵ). So check parallelism first, then substitute t = 1 s.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">r = u·t + ½·a·t²</span> (m, from the origin) and <span class="eq">v = u + a·t</span> (m s⁻¹), applied component-wise. Speed <span class="eq">|v| = √(vₓ² + v_y²)</span>. Parallel test: <b>u ∥ a ⟺ uₓ/aₓ = u_y/a_y</b>. <span class="why">Units: a·t = m s⁻² × s = m s⁻¹ ✓ and ½at² = m ✓.</span> <b>Assumption:</b> a = î + 4ĵ is constant in m s⁻², and the particle starts at the origin at t = 0.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Data: u = <b>(2î + 3ĵ) m s⁻¹</b>, a = <b>(î + 4ĵ) m s⁻²</b>, r₀ = 0 at t = 0.</div>
<div class="step">2. <b>Test (a) vs (b)</b> — is a parallel to u? Compare component ratios: uₓ/aₓ = 2/1 = <b>2</b>, while u_y/a_y = 3/4 = <b>0.75</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. The ratios differ (2 ≠ 0.75), so <b>a is not parallel to u</b>: it has a component perpendicular to the initial velocity, which steers the particle sideways.<span class="why">Constant acceleration with a transverse component ⇒ the trajectory is a parabola, exactly like a projectile.</span></div>
<div class="step">4. Therefore <b>(a) "path is parabolic" is correct</b> and <b>(b) "path is a straight line" is wrong</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. <b>Check (c)</b> — position at t = 1 s: r = ut + ½at² = (2î + 3ĵ)(1 s) + ½(î + 4ĵ)(1 s)².</div>
<div class="step">6. First term = (2î + 3ĵ) m; second term = ½(î + 4ĵ) = (0.5î + 2ĵ) m.</div>
<div class="step">7. Add component-wise: r = (2 + 0.5)î + (3 + 2)ĵ = <b>(2.5î + 5ĵ) m</b> ⟹ <b>(c) is correct</b>.</div>
<div class="step">8. <b>Check (d)</b> — velocity at t = 1 s: v = u + at = (2î + 3ĵ) + (î + 4ĵ)(1) = <b>(3î + 7ĵ) m s⁻¹</b>.</div>
<div class="step">9. Speed = |v| = √(3² + 7²) = √(9 + 49) = <b>√58 m s⁻¹ ≈ 7.6 m s⁻¹</b> ⟹ <b>(d) is correct</b>.</div>
<div class="step">10. Sanity check: the initial speed was |u| = √(4+9) = √13 ≈ 3.6 m s⁻¹, so the particle has sped up under the acceleration — consistent.</div>
<pre>
 option                         working                        verdict
 (a) path parabolic       2/1 ≠ 3/4 ⇒ a ∦ u, a is constant     ✓
 (b) path straight line   would need a ∥ u                     ✗
 (c) r(1s) = 2.5î + 5ĵ    (2î+3ĵ)(1) + ½(î+4ĵ)(1)²             ✓
 (d) speed(1s) = √58      v = 3î+7ĵ ⇒ √(9+49) = √58            ✓
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> The <b>straight-vs-curved test is a one-line ratio check</b>: u ∥ a ⇒ straight, otherwise parabolic (for constant a). No need to eliminate t and derive y(x). Also note (a) and (b) are mutually exclusive, so one is free once the other is settled. Trap: with vectors, <b>speed ≠ sum of components</b> — |v| = √(3²+7²) = √58 ≈ 7.6, not 3 + 7 = 10; and remember ½at² applies to the <i>vector</i> a, halving both of its components.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (a) path is parabolic, (c) position at t = 1 s is (2.5î + 5ĵ) m, and (d) speed at t = 1 s is √58 m s⁻¹.</div></div></details></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<h2>Level 6</h2>
<div class="tablecard"><div class="scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Topic</th><th>YouTube</th><th>Match</th><th class="ans-h">Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="num">1</td><td class="q">A particle is projected with velocity v₀ along the x-axis; deceleration a = αx² (proportional to square of distance from origin). Distance at which it stops: (a) √(3v₀/2α) (b) (3v₀/2α)^(1/3) (c) √(2v₀²/3α) (d) (3v₀²/2α)^(1/3)</td><td class="topic">Position-dependent deceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gzL2MWMeEOc" target="_blank">A particle is projected with velocity V₀ along x-axis. The deceleration on the particle is propo...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval flagged"><span class="lt">b</span>(3v₀/2α)^(1/3) <span class="warn" title="The key says (b), and (b) really is printed as (3v₀/2α)^⅓ — re-verified at 8× zoom on the PDF (p.156), v₀ to the FIRST power. That cannot be right: it is not even dimensionally a length. Integrating the given a = −αx² as v dv/dx = −αx² gives −v₀²/2 = −αx³/3, so x_stop = (3v₀²/2α)^⅓ — which is the printed option (d), not (b). So the ANSWER KEY's letter is wrong (the option list itself is fine, so this is not an option-order mismatch). Found by inspecting the question: this row has no worked solution to compare against.">⚠</span></span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">2</td><td class="q">A street car moves from station A to B (rest to rest) with acceleration f = a − bx (a, b constants; x = distance from A). Find the distance between stations and the maximum velocity</td><td class="topic">Position-dependent acceleration, v dv/dx</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WsmD3w41vQ" target="_blank">A street car moves rectilinearly from station A to the next station B (from rest to rest)...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">a</span>x = 2a/b, v_max = a/√b</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3</td><td class="q">A particle of mass m moves in a line under force F = −mk sin 3t (k &gt; 0); at t = 0 it is at O with velocity u. Find the acceleration, velocity and displacement from O at time t</td><td class="topic">Time-dependent force, double integration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=spaRpldBUmM" target="_blank">A particle of mass m moving along a straight line experiences force F which varies with the distance</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">−k sin 3t, (1/3k)(cos 3t − 1) + u, (k/9)(sin 3t − 3t) + ut</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> The force depends on <b>time</b> (not on position or velocity), which is the friendliest case: Newton's second law hands you a(t) directly, and then velocity and displacement follow by <b>integrating twice with respect to t</b>. The mass cancels immediately — a useful sign that the trajectory does not depend on how heavy the particle is. The one thing that must not be skipped is the <b>constant of integration</b> at each stage; those constants are what carry the initial conditions (v = u and x = 0 at t = 0) into the answer.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a = F ÷ m</span> (m s⁻²); <span class="eq">v = ∫a dt + C₁</span> (m s⁻¹); <span class="eq">x = ∫v dt + C₂</span> (m). Standard integrals: <span class="eq">∫sin 3t dt = −(1/3)cos 3t</span> and <span class="eq">∫cos 3t dt = (1/3)sin 3t</span>. <span class="why">Each integration with respect to t divides by the 3 inside the argument, via the chain rule.</span> <b>Assumptions:</b> the motion is along one straight line (so signed scalars suffice), k &gt; 0 is a constant, and the initial conditions are x = 0 (at O) and v = u at t = 0.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. <b>Acceleration.</b> Newton's second law: a = F ÷ m = (−mk sin 3t) ÷ m = <b>−k sin 3t</b> (m s⁻²).<span class="why">The mass cancels, so the resulting motion is independent of m.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. <b>Velocity.</b> Integrate a with respect to t: v = ∫(−k sin 3t) dt = −k × [−(1/3)cos 3t] + C₁ = <b>(k/3)cos 3t + C₁</b>.</div>
<div class="step">3. Apply the initial condition v = u at t = 0, where cos 0 = 1: u = (k/3)(1) + C₁ ⟹ <b>C₁ = u − k/3</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. So the velocity is v = (k/3)cos 3t + u − k/3, i.e. <b>v = u − (k/3)(1 − cos 3t)</b> (m s⁻¹).<span class="why">Since 1 − cos 3t ≥ 0 always, the particle's velocity never exceeds u — the force only ever subtracts from it.</span></div>
<div class="step">5. <b>Displacement.</b> Integrate v with respect to t: x = ∫[(u − k/3) + (k/3)cos 3t] dt = (u − k/3)t + (k/3) × (1/3)sin 3t + C₂.</div>
<div class="step">6. That gives x = (u − k/3)t + (k/9)sin 3t + C₂.</div>
<div class="step">7. Apply x = 0 at t = 0 (the particle is at O), where sin 0 = 0: 0 = 0 + 0 + C₂ ⟹ <b>C₂ = 0</b>.</div>
<div class="step">8. Therefore <b>x = (u − k/3)t + (k/9)sin 3t</b> (m).</div>
<div class="step">9. Verify by differentiating back: dx/dt = (u − k/3) + (k/9)(3cos 3t) = (u − k/3) + (k/3)cos 3t = v ✓, and dv/dt = −(k/3)(3 sin 3t) = −k sin 3t = a ✓. At t = 0: x = 0 ✓, v = u ✓.</div>
<pre>
 F = −mk sin 3t
      │ ÷ m
      ▼
 a(t) = −k sin 3t                        [m/s²]
      │ ∫dt  ,  v(0) = u
      ▼
 v(t) = u − (k/3)(1 − cos 3t)            [m/s]
      │ ∫dt  ,  x(0) = 0
      ▼
 x(t) = (u − k/3)t + (k/9) sin 3t        [m]
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> When the force is a function of <b>time</b>, integrate straight down the ladder <b>a → v → x</b> and apply the initial condition <i>immediately after each integration</i> — evaluating both constants at the end is where sign errors breed. Watch the inner constant: each ∫ of sin 3t or cos 3t brings a factor <b>1/3</b>, so the displacement picks up k/9 = (k/3)(1/3). Trap: if the force instead depended on <b>x</b> or <b>v</b>, this method collapses and you must use <span class="eq">a = v·dv/dx</span> and separate the variables (see Level 6 Q2 and Q9).</div>
<div class="final">Key point: a = −k sin 3t m s⁻²; v = u − (k/3)(1 − cos 3t) m s⁻¹ [= (k/3)cos 3t + u − k/3]; x = (u − k/3)t + (k/9) sin 3t m.</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">4</td><td class="q">Momentum of a particle in a straight line is p = ln t + 1/t (in m/s). Find the time (t &gt; 0) when the net force is zero, and the momentum at that time</td><td class="topic">F = dp/dt, stationary point</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CunBn1oscEQ" target="_blank">The momentum of a moving particle given by p = t ln t. Net force acting on this particle is defined...</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval flagged">1 kg m/sec. <span class="warn" title="Answer-key value 1 kg m/sec, and our solution agrees (dp/dt = 1/t − 1/t² = 0 ⇒ t = 1 s; p = ln1 + 1/1 = 1). The book's UNIT is the defect, now confirmed against the PDF (p.156): it really prints 'p = ln t + 1/t (in m/s)', but m/s is a velocity — momentum is kg·m·s⁻¹. Note the answer key itself writes 'kg m/sec', contradicting the book's own '(in m/s)'. Book defect, confirmed.">⚠</span></span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Newton's second law in its original and most general form says the <b>net force is the rate of change of momentum</b>, F = dp/dt. So "net force is zero" translates directly into "dp/dt = 0" — the condition for a <b>stationary point</b> of p(t). The question is therefore a calculus problem wearing a physics costume: differentiate p, set the derivative to zero, solve for t, then substitute back to get p at that instant.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">F = dp/dt</span>; setting F = 0 gives <span class="eq">dp/dt = 0</span>. Derivatives needed: <span class="eq">d(ln t)/dt = 1/t</span> and <span class="eq">d(t⁻¹)/dt = −1/t²</span>. <span class="why">A zero derivative means p is momentarily neither rising nor falling — momentum is instantaneously constant, which is exactly what "no net force" means.</span> <b>Assumption / flag:</b> the book prints the units of p as "(in m/s)", which is dimensionally wrong — momentum is <b>kg m s⁻¹</b>. The numerical answer is unaffected; only the label is off.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Given p = ln t + 1/t, valid for t &gt; 0.<span class="why">ln t is undefined for t ≤ 0, and 1/t blows up at t = 0 — hence the stated restriction.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Write the force as the derivative: F = dp/dt = d(ln t)/dt + d(t⁻¹)/dt.</div>
<div class="step">3. Differentiate term by term: d(ln t)/dt = <b>1/t</b>, and d(t⁻¹)/dt = <b>−1/t²</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. So F = 1/t − 1/t².</div>
<div class="step">5. Set the net force to zero: 1/t − 1/t² = 0 ⟹ 1/t = 1/t².</div>
<div class="step">6. Multiply both sides by t² (legal since t &gt; 0, so t² ≠ 0): t²/t = t²/t² ⟹ <b>t = 1 s</b>.</div>
<div class="step">7. Substitute t = 1 s back into p: p = ln(1) + 1/1 = 0 + 1 = <b>1</b> (in SI, 1 kg m s⁻¹).<span class="why">ln 1 = 0 because e⁰ = 1.</span></div>
<div class="step">8. Confirm it is a genuine stationary point by checking the sign of F either side: at t = 0.5, F = 2 − 4 = <b>−2</b> (&lt; 0, p falling); at t = 2, F = 0.5 − 0.25 = <b>+0.25</b> (&gt; 0, p rising). The force changes sign through zero at t = 1 s ✓ — so p is at a <b>minimum</b> there.</div>
<pre>
 p(t) = ln t + 1/t          F(t) = dp/dt = 1/t − 1/t²

 t       0.5      1.0      2.0
 F      −2.00     0.00    +0.25      ← sign change ⇒ genuine root
 p       1.31     1.00     1.19      ← minimum of p at t = 1 s

 F = 0 ⇒ 1/t = 1/t² ⇒ t = 1 s ,  p(1) = ln1 + 1 = 1
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Read <b>F = dp/dt</b> as the master form of Newton's second law — F = ma is only its special case for constant mass. So <b>"net force = 0" ⟺ "p is stationary" ⟺ maximum/minimum of p(t)</b>, converting force questions into pure differentiation. Trap: dividing 1/t = 1/t² by t without noting t &gt; 0 can smuggle in a bogus root at t = 0; the domain of ln t excludes it anyway. Note also that F = 0 does <b>not</b> mean the particle is at rest — its momentum is 1 kg m s⁻¹, merely not changing at that instant.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: t = 1 s, at which the momentum p = 1 (SI: 1 kg m s⁻¹).</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">5</td><td class="q">A particle with uniform acceleration has average velocities v₁, v₂, v₃ over successive intervals t₁, t₂, t₃. Find (v₁−v₂)/(v₂−v₃): (a) (t₁−t₂)/(t₂−t₃) (b) (t₁−t₂)/(t₂+t₃) (c) (t₁+t₂)/(t₂−t₃) (d) (t₁+t₂)/(t₂+t₃)</td><td class="topic">Average velocity over unequal intervals</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aWWc2L_qYOs" target="_blank">A particle moving with uniform acceleration has average velocities V1, V2 and V3 over the suc...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">d</span>(t₁ + t₂)/(t₂ + t₃)</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">6</td><td class="q">A juggler throws one ball whenever the previous is at its highest point, throwing n balls per second. If g is the acceleration due to gravity and the height is g/(Kn²), find K</td><td class="topic">Free-fall timing between throws</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3JBHGkCScx8" target="_blank">A juggler throws balls into air. He throws one whenever the previous one is at its highest point...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">2</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">7</td><td class="q">A particle with velocity 45 ms⁻¹ at t=0 decelerates at |a| = α√v. Find the distance travelled before it comes to rest: (a) S = 3v₀^(3/2)/2α (b) S = 5v₀^(3/2)/2α (c) S = 2v₀^(3/2)/3α (d) S = 2v₀^(3/2)/5α</td><td class="topic">Velocity-dependent deceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LfUmNTsx9GQ" target="_blank">A particle having a velocity v = v₀ at t = 0 is decelerated at the rate |a| = α√v...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>S = (2/3)(v₀^(3/2)/α)</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">8</td><td class="q">A particle on the x-axis has acceleration f = f₀(1 − t/T) (f₀, T constants), zero velocity at t=0. Find the velocity vₓ when f = 0: (a) ½f₀T² (b) f₀T² (c) f₀T/2 (d) f₀T</td><td class="topic">Integrate time-varying acceleration</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CXhKsUXmbDo" target="_blank">A particle moving along x-axis has acceleration f, at time t given by...</a></td><td><span class="badge exact">🟢 Exact</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval"><span class="lt">c</span>f₀T/2</span></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">9</td><td class="q">Velocity of a particle in a straight line depends on position as v = 3 sin(x + π/2) m/s. Find the acceleration at x = π/4</td><td class="topic">a = v·dv/dx</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E9kUfmARxEA" target="_blank">The velocity v of a particle moving along x-axis varies with position x as v = α√x</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval">−9/2 m/s²</span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> Here velocity is given as a function of <b>position</b>, not of time, so a = dv/dt cannot be used directly — you do not have v(t). The bridge is the chain rule: <span class="eq">a = dv/dt = (dv/dx)(dx/dt) = v·(dv/dx)</span>. This "v dv/dx" form is the standard weapon whenever v depends on x, and it turns the problem into a single differentiation followed by a substitution. A useful simplification comes first: sin(x + π/2) is just <b>cos x</b>, so v = 3 cos x.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">a = v·(dv/dx)</span> — v in m s⁻¹, x in m, a in m s⁻². <span class="why">Units: (m s⁻¹) × (m s⁻¹ ÷ m) = m s⁻², so the combination really is an acceleration.</span> Identities used: <span class="eq">sin(x + π/2) = cos x</span>, <span class="eq">d(cos x)/dx = −sin x</span>, and <span class="eq">2 sin x cos x = sin 2x</span>. <b>Assumption:</b> the argument of the sine is in <b>radians</b> and the constant 3 carries the unit m s⁻¹, so x is a length in metres.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. Simplify the given velocity: v = 3 sin(x + π/2) = <b>3 cos x</b> (m s⁻¹).<span class="why">Shifting a sine by π/2 gives a cosine — this removes the phase and makes differentiating trivial.</span></div>
<div class="step">2. Since v is a function of x (not t), use the chain-rule form a = v·(dv/dx).</div>
<div class="step">3. Differentiate v with respect to x: dv/dx = d(3 cos x)/dx = <b>−3 sin x</b> (s⁻¹).</div>
<div class="step">4. Form the product: a = v × (dv/dx) = (3 cos x) × (−3 sin x) = <b>−9 sin x cos x</b>.</div>
<div class="step">5. Compact it with the double-angle identity: −9 sin x cos x = <b>−(9/2)·sin 2x</b> (m s⁻²).</div>
<div class="step">6. Evaluate at x = π/4: sin 2x = sin(2 × π/4) = sin(π/2) = <b>1</b>.</div>
<div class="step">7. So a = −(9/2) × 1 = <b>−4.5 m s⁻²</b>.<span class="why">Direct check without the identity: at x = π/4, sin x = cos x = 1/√2, so a = −9 × (1/√2)(1/√2) = −9 × ½ = −4.5 m s⁻² ✓.</span></div>
<div class="step">8. Interpret the sign: the acceleration is <b>4.5 m s⁻² in the −x direction</b>, i.e. the particle is decelerating (at x = π/4, v = 3cos(π/4) = +2.12 m s⁻¹ &gt; 0, so v and a oppose).</div>
<pre>
 v(x) = 3 sin(x + π/2) = 3 cos x        [m/s]
 dv/dx = −3 sin x                       [1/s]

 a = v·dv/dx = (3 cos x)(−3 sin x)
            = −9 sin x cos x = −(9/2) sin 2x     [m/s²]

 at x = π/4 :  sin 2x = sin(π/2) = 1
               a = −9/2 = −4.5 m/s²   (magnitude 4.5, along −x)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Match the form of the data to the tool: <b>v(t) ⇒ a = dv/dt; v(x) ⇒ a = v·dv/dx</b>. That single rule handles every position-dependent-velocity item in this chapter. Bonus shortcut: for v² = f(x), <span class="eq">a = ½·d(v²)/dx</span> — here v² = 9cos²x, so a = ½(−18 cos x sin x) = −9 sin x cos x, the same answer with no product rule. Trap: writing a = dv/dx alone (forgetting the factor v) is the standard error, and it is not even dimensionally an acceleration.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: a = −4.5 m s⁻² (magnitude 4.5 m s⁻², directed along −x).</div></div></details></td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">10</td><td class="q">*(Multiple correct)* For constant a, compare Sₙ (displacement in nth second) and average velocity over [0,t]: (a) Sₙ = u + ½a(2n−1) (b) v̄ = s/t = (u+v)/2 (c) if a&lt;0, Sₙ must be negative for large n (d) if u=0, Sₙ is an arithmetic progression in n</td><td class="topic">Sₙ formula &amp; average-velocity concepts</td><td><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=em0m5MnHvTU" target="_blank">Displacement in nth second, Average velocity - constant acceleration| PHYSICS | CBSE | IITJEE | NEET</a></td><td><span class="badge sim">🟡 Similar</span></td><td class="ans"><span class="ansval flagged"><span class="lt">abd</span>Sₙ = u + ½a(2n − 1) · v̄ = s/t = (u+v)/2 · If u = 0, Sₙ is an arithmetic progression in n <span class="warn" title="Key letters (a)(b)(d) — our solution agrees on all three. The disagreement is over (c) 'if a &lt; 0, Sₙ must be negative for large n': the book excludes it, while OUR worked solution argues it is defensible as pure kinematics (Sₙ = u + ½a(2n−1) &lt; 0 once n &gt; ½ + u/|a|). Read as our overreach against the book's intent, not a book defect; displayed letters are the key's.">⚠</span></span></td></tr>
<tr class="solnrow"><td colspan="6"><details class="soln"><summary></summary><div class="solnbody"><div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Concept</span> A concept-check on two staples of constant-acceleration motion. <b>Sₙ</b> is the displacement during the nth second — obtained by subtracting the displacement in (n−1) seconds from that in n seconds, so despite appearances it is a <i>distance</i> (metres), not a velocity. <b>Average velocity</b> over [0, t] is always total displacement ÷ total time; for constant a it additionally equals the mean of the end velocities, ½(u+v), because v(t) is linear in t. Each option is tested against these two derivations.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Formula</span> <span class="eq">Sₙ = s(n) − s(n−1) = u + ½·a·(2n − 1)</span> — the displacement in the nth second (m), with u in m s⁻¹, a in m s⁻², n a pure number. <span class="why">The "unit" of the (2n−1) term is 1 second: the formula is shorthand for [u + ½a(2n−1)] × (1 s), which is why a velocity-looking expression returns metres.</span> And <span class="eq">v̄ = s/t = ½(u + v)</span> (m s⁻¹), valid only for constant a. <b>Assumption throughout:</b> a is constant and the motion is rectilinear.</div>
<div class="seg"><span class="lbl">Solution</span>
<div class="step">1. <b>Derive Sₙ.</b> Displacement in n seconds: s(n) = un + ½an². In (n−1) seconds: s(n−1) = u(n−1) + ½a(n−1)².</div>
<div class="step">2. Subtract: Sₙ = [un + ½an²] − [u(n−1) + ½a(n−1)²] = u[n − (n−1)] + ½a[n² − (n−1)²].</div>
<div class="step">3. Simplify: n − (n−1) = 1, and n² − (n−1)² = n² − (n² − 2n + 1) = <b>2n − 1</b>.</div>
<div class="step">4. So Sₙ = u + ½·a·(2n − 1) ⟹ <b>(a) is correct</b>.<span class="why">Check with u = 0, a = 2, n = 3: S₃ = 0 + ½(2)(5) = 5 m; direct: s(3) − s(2) = 9 − 4 = 5 m ✓.</span></div>
<div class="step">5. <b>Test (b).</b> Average velocity is by definition v̄ = s/t. For constant a, s = ut + ½at², so s/t = u + ½at.</div>
<div class="step">6. Also v = u + at ⟹ ½(u + v) = ½(u + u + at) = u + ½at — the <b>same expression</b>. So v̄ = s/t = (u+v)/2 ⟹ <b>(b) is correct</b>.<span class="why">This equality holds because v(t) is linear under constant a; it fails for non-uniform acceleration.</span></div>
<div class="step">7. <b>Test (c).</b> With a &lt; 0, Sₙ = u + ½a(2n − 1) decreases without bound as n grows, since the term ½a(2n−1) → −∞. Solving Sₙ &lt; 0 gives n &gt; ½ + u/|a|, so beyond that value every Sₙ is negative ⟹ <b>(c) is correct</b>, <i>provided</i> the constant acceleration genuinely persists (see the flag below).</div>
<div class="step">8. Read (c) physically: a negative Sₙ simply means that by the nth second the body has reversed and is now moving backwards — e.g. u = 5 m s⁻¹, a = −2 m s⁻²: S₁ = 4 m, S₂ = 2 m, S₃ = 0 m, S₄ = −2 m.</div>
<div class="step">9. <b>Test (d).</b> With u = 0: Sₙ = ½a(2n − 1) = a·n − a/2, which is <b>linear in n</b>. Successive terms differ by Sₙ₊₁ − Sₙ = a — a constant common difference, the definition of an arithmetic progression ⟹ <b>(d) is correct</b>.</div>
<div class="step">10. For u = 0 the sequence runs ½a, (3/2)a, (5/2)a, (7/2)a, … — the familiar <b>1 : 3 : 5 : 7</b> odd-number ratio, which is an AP with common difference a ✓.</div>
<pre>
 option                              test                              verdict
 (a) Sₙ = u + ½a(2n−1)     s(n) − s(n−1) ⇒ u + ½a(2n−1)                ✓
 (b) v̄ = s/t = (u+v)/2     s/t = u + ½at = ½(u + [u+at])               ✓
 (c) a&lt;0 ⇒ Sₙ &lt; 0 large n  Sₙ &lt; 0 once n &gt; ½ + u/|a|                   ✓*
 (d) u=0 ⇒ Sₙ is an AP     Sₙ = a·n − a/2, common difference = a       ✓

 * true as pure kinematics, i.e. if a really stays constant for all n
   example (u=5, a=−2):  S₁=4, S₂=2, S₃=0, S₄=−2, S₅=−4 m  (body reverses)
</pre>
</div>
<div class="trick"><span class="lbl">⚡ JEE Trick</span> Remember <span class="eq">Sₙ = u + ½a(2n−1)</span> and its consequence — from rest, successive-second distances are in the ratio <b>1 : 3 : 5 : 7</b> (an AP), which cracks a whole family of problems instantly. Trap: Sₙ <i>looks</i> like a velocity because of the "u + …" form, but it is a <b>displacement in metres</b> — the hidden ×(1 s) is what fixes the units. Statement (c) is the subtle one: it is sound as pure kinematics, but a real body decelerating at constant a would normally stop rather than accelerate backwards forever — the option is true only under the idealisation that a is maintained indefinitely.</div>
<div class="final">Answer: (a), (b) and (d) are correct; (c) is also correct as pure kinematics (Sₙ &lt; 0 once n &gt; ½ + u/|a|), on the idealisation that the constant negative acceleration persists indefinitely.</div></div></details></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></div>

<footer>Generated for ICAD PhyV1 · Module P4 · Lesson L2. Exact-match videos solve the identical question; "Similar" videos teach the same method with different numbers.</footer>
</div>
<script>
(function(){
  var btn = document.getElementById('ansBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', function(){
    var on = document.body.classList.toggle('show-ans');
    btn.textContent = on ? '🙈 Hide Answers' : '🔑 Show Answers';
  });
  // click a single blurred answer to reveal just that one
  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    var t = e.target;
    if (t && t.classList && t.classList.contains('warn')) return;
    var host = t && t.closest ? t.closest('.ansval') : null;
    if (host && !document.body.classList.contains('show-ans')){
      host.classList.toggle('revealed');
    }
  });
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
